{ Can White win the game ? } 1. Rf6
{ White is spoilt for choice in this endgame. The main idea is to sacrifice the pawn to keep the king in the wrong corner. Placing the rook on f6 is ideal for this purpose. }
1... Bc1
{ A waiting move. Another idea is to annoy the rook with the king; this once occurred in an even more famous game:   69...Kg7  (in this game actually move 87)  70.Kf5  Be3  71.h6+ !  Bxh6  72.Rg6+ !  Kh7  73.Kf6 !  (White's king reaches the winning square)  Be3  74.Kf7  Ba7  75.Ra6  Bb8  76.Ra8  Bc7  77.Rc8  Bf4  78.Rc4  Bg5  79.Rc3  1-0 Tal-Zhidkov, Baku 1972. }
2. h6 Bxh6 3. Kh5 Be3 4. Rf7+ Kg8 5. Kg6
{ Black's king is trapped in the wrong corner. White's task, besides making sure the enemy king doesn't get out, is to set up a double attack by threatening to capture the bishop as well as delivering checkmate. }
5... Bg1
{ This position has occurred in twenty-five games in my database. The winning manoeuvre was carried out in all of them. }
6. Rf1 Bh2 7. Rf2 Bg3 8. Rg2 Bd6 9. Rd2 Be7 10. Ra2 { 1-0.  See Ending 6. } *

