{ Can White draw the game by exchanging on c4 ? } 1. Bxc4
{ Exchanging on c4 is a mistake. Spotting Black's subtle and only winning manoeuvre is not easy, though.   63.Ke2 !  should hold the draw. If  b3  then  64.Bxc4  is now correct:  Kxc4  65.Kd2  Kd4  66.h4  h5  67.Kc1  Ke4  68.Kb2  Kf3  69.Kxb3  Kxf2  70.Kc3  Kg3  71.Kd3  Kxh4  72.Ke2  Kg3  73.Kf1 ! = }
1... Kxc4 2. Kd2 Kb3
{ A surprising move: Black's king will still be in time to capture both enemy pawns before settling on the g2-square. }
3. f4 Kc4 4. f5 Kd5 5. Kd3 Ke5 6. Kc4 Kxf5 7. Kxb4 Kg5 8. Kc3 Kh4 9. Kd3 Kxh3
10. Ke3 { 72.Ke2  Kg2 !  -+ } 10... Kg3 { 0-1.  See Ending 5. } *

