{ Can Black save the knight ? } 1... Ka3
{ When the only option for a knight to stick close to its king is to jump onto the knight's most awkward square on the board  (here, b2)  , the practical result is usually defeat:  These positions are extremely difficult, as is evidenced by the large number of errors from this moment onwards. The correct move was   95...Kb3 !  96.Rb7+  Ka3  , transposing to move 97 in the game. }
2. Ra7+
{ This only helps White reach a safe position.   96.Rf3  traps the knight by force, for example:  Ka2   96...Nb2+  97.Kc3  Na4+  98.Kc4+  Ka2  99.Kb4  transposes  97.Rf1  Nb2+  98.Kc3  Na4+  99.Kb4  Nb2  100.Rh1  Nd3+  101.Kc3  (the knight must wander off)  Nf4  102.Rd1  (not allowing the knight to return via the d5-square)  Ne6  103.Rd5  Nf4  104.Re5 !  (a further step towards restraint)  Kb1  105.Re1+  Ka2  106.Kc2  Nd5  107.Re5  Nb4+  108.Kc3  Nc6  109.Re6 !  (finally, the knight is totally dominated)  Nd8  110.Rd6  Nf7  111.Rd2+  Ka3  112.Rd7  +-  . }
2... Kb3 3. Rb7+ Ka3 4. Rb8 Nb2+ 5. Kc2 Na4 6. Rb3+ Ka2 7. Rb5 Ka3 8. Kd3
{ The crucial moment. } 8... Ka2
{ Errs again, when the knight is one jump away from its most awkward square, which we called 'dumb square' in 100 Endgames You Must Know.   102...Nb2+ !  103.Kd4  Na4  104.Kc4  Nb2+  105.Kd4  Na4  keeps up the defence. }
9. Kc4
{ White now has a theoretical win. For the remainder of the game, only the critical errors are pointed out. }
9... Nb2+ 10. Kc3 Na4+ 11. Kb4 Nb2 12. Rd5 Kb1 13. Kb3 { 107.Ka3 !  +-  . }
13... Kc1 14. Rc5+ Kb1 15. Rc3 Nd1 16. Rc8 Nf2 17. Rf8 Nd1 18. Rc8 Nf2 19. Rc2
Nd1 20. Re2 Kc1 21. Kc4 Kb1 { 115...Nb2+ ! =  . Again the 'dumb' square. } 22.
Kd3 Nb2+ 23. Kc3 Nd1+ 24. Kd2 Nb2 25. Re8 Ka2 26. Kc3 Na4+ 27. Kb4 Nb2 28. Rb8
Nd1 29. Rd8 Ne3 30. Kc3 Kb1 31. Rd3 Nf5 32. Rf3 Nd6 33. Rf4 Ka2 34. Kb4 Kb2 35.
Kc5 Nb7+ 36. Kb6 Nd6 37. Kc6 Nc8 38. Rf7 Kb3 39. Rc7
{ 1-0.  See Ending 9, Kamsky-Bacrot. } *

