{ Should Black play ...a4-a3 ? } 1... a3
{ The move ...a4-a3 is a fatal blunder, allowing checkmate with a lone knight. This is an easy example, but it is striking to see how the offside knight still manages to give checkmate.  The logical   63...Ka3  leads to a draw:  64.Kc3  Ka2 = }
2. Nf3 Ka1 3. Nd2
{ 65.Nd4  Ka2  66.Ne2  Ka1  67.Nc1  a2  68.Nb3#  was the shortest route to victory. }
3... Ka2 4. Ne4 Ka1 5. Nc5 Ka2 6. Nd3 Ka1 7. Nc1 a2 8. Nb3# { See Ending 14. }
