{ Can Black win the game ? } 1... a4
{ is the right move. It's perhaps counter-intuitive that the knight, apparently closer to the action on the e4-square, is in fact too slow:   64...g3 ?  Black is winning, but not with this move. Now the knight is able to make it back in time, even though in the game it failed to do so.  65.Nxg3  a4  66.Ne4 ?  White doesn't seem to know about the rule of the correct knight circuit:   66.Ne2 !  (toward the c1-square)  and if  Kb2  67.Nf4 !  (toward the d3-square)  Kc3   67...a3  68.Nd3+ =  68.Nd5+ !  Kb3  69.Nf4  a3  70.Nd3 =  66...a3 !  Now there is no way to get on track.  67.Nc3+   67.Nd2  Ka1  68.Nc4  a2  69.Kg4  Kb1  -+  67...Kb2  68.Nd1+  Kb3  -+  0-1.  80 }
2. Kxg4
{ 65.Nc3+  Kb3  66.Nxa4  g3 !  Now the other pawn does a runner.  67.Nc5+  Ka3 !  -+  Again we see the optimal range of king vis-à-vis knight. }
2... a3 3. Nd2 { Intending to get on track. } 3... Ka1
{ Truly remarkable, yet at the same time completely logical: by stepping into the corner, the Black king renders the knight's route via c4 useless. Only after playing ...a3-a2 will it step out of the corner to facilitate promotion, except in the variation }
4. Ne4 Kb2 { See Ending 12. } *

