[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/1K6/1P6/8/3n4/5kp1/1R6/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Can Black save the game ? } 1... g2
{ Here, we find a perfect example of how to share duties: the g3-pawn, supported by the king, must force a rook sacrifice, while the knight aims to give itself up for the b6-pawn. Both these tasks are feasible, but Black must play accurately. After 62...g2, the knight will not be able to control the b-pawn.  It was necessary to play   62...Ne6 !  and the knight would get the job done without any problems. }
2. Rxg2 Kxg2 3. Kc8
{ This would be the correct move.   64.Kb8 ?  White returns the favour with this inaccurate move.  Kf3 ??  A losing blunder. The player with the Black pieces was probably unfamiliar not only with the concept of lateral control, but also with the specific features of knight's pawns.   64...Nc2  65.b7  Nb4   64...Nf5 ??  65.Kc7   64...Ne6 =  65.b7  Nb5  66.Ka8 !  Nc7+  67.Ka7  1-0. }
3... Nb5 4. Kd7
{ The same move would follow after 64... Nf5; two further examples of effectively fending off an enemy knight.  See Ending 10 & Ending 11. }
