{ White chose to go to e3 with his king. How should Black react? } 1. Ke3
{ It might appear irrelevant where White's king goes, but if he chooses the wrong option, Black can prevent the White pawn from promoting and is able, by doing some extraordinary twisting, to get the king in to harass the knight. The move played in the game is wrong because the king is useless here. It ought to be helping the advance of the f-pawn:   51.Ke5 !  Kg3  52.f4  h4  if now   52...Kg4  53.f5  Kg5  54.f6  Kg6  55.Ke6  and Black even loses  53.f5  h3  54.f6  h2  55.f7  h1=Q  56.f8=Q  Qh5+  57.Kd4  Qd1+  58.Kc3  a1=Q+ = }
1... Kg3 2. f4 Kg4
{ 52...h4 ?  is a race which ends in both pawns queening:  53.f5  h3  54.f6  h2  55.f7  h1=Q  56.f8=Q = }
3. Na1
{ 53.Ke4 ?  is a bad idea, allowing the Black pawn to queen with check:  h4  54.f5  h3  55.f6  h2  56.f7  h1=Q+ }
3... h4 4. Nc2
{ 54.Nb3  leads to a different variation, with a different no-go area for the king:  h3  55.Kf2  Kxf4  56.Kg1  Now the king can easily overcome this hindrance.  Ke5  57.Kh2  Kd5 !  58.Kxh3  Kc4  59.Na1  Kc3  -+ }
4... h3 5. Kf2 Kxf4 6. Na1 Ke5
{ There are other winning moves, but Black already sets out to circumvent the no-go area about to be concocted by the knight. }
7. Nc2 { The squares e3, d3, d4 and d5 represent the no-go area for the king. }
7... Kd6
{ 0-1. Black's king easily dismantles the barrier and is in time to bully the knight. Any move not directly aimed to circumvent the no-go area in the most efficient way spoils the win, for example:   57...Ke4 ?  58.Kg3  Ke5  59.Kxh3  Kd6  60.Kg3  Kc5  61.Kf3  Kc4  62.Ke2  Kc3  63.Kd1  and White's king is in time to help the knight.   See Ending 13. }
*

