{ Choose between 55. Rd1+ and 55. Rg1. } 1. Rg1
{ is the right move. The forcing variation that Black has at his disposal allows us to witness an astonishing display of resilience, worthy of a Troitsky study.   55.Rd1+ ?  Black threatens 55... Bg2, winning, but White can prevent this move by playing 55. Rg1, and later on try to win the bishop with a knight fork. There is, however, a crucial difference between playing 55. Rg1 directly, or flicking in a check. It seems logical to push the king away, but White is actually helping his opponent get out of any knight forks.  Kc2  56.Rg1  Bg2  57.Rxg2  Rxg2  58.Ne1+  Kb3 !  On this remote square, the king can't be disturbed.  59.Nxg2  h3  Finally, the knight sits helpless on its most uncomfortable square on the board, and while it is true that the king comes to its aid, the pawn endgame is lost.  60.Kg3  hxg2  61.Kxg2  Kc4  0-1. }
1... Bg2
{ Here, this is not the best move as it allows a forced draw :   55...Bf1 !  would have made White suffer a little while longer, but the result should be a draw, too, for example:  56.Rg6  h3  57.Rd6+  Kc4  58.Kg3  Rg2+  59.Kxh3  Rg7+  60.Kh4  Kxc5  =+ }
2. Rxg2 Rxg2 3. Ne1+
{ And now Black's king has a choice between no less than 5 squares, but believe it or not, all allow a tempo-gaining knight check: }
3... Ke2
{ 57...Kd2  58.Nxg2  h3  59.Nh4  h2  60.Nf3+ =   57...Kd4  58.Nxg2  h3  59.Nh4  h2  60.Nf3+ =   57...Kc3  58.Nxg2  h3  59.Ne3  h2  60.Nd1+  Kd4  61.Nf2 =   57...Kc4  58.Nxg2  h3  59.Ne3+  Kxc5  60.Kg3 = }
4. Nxg2 h3 5. Ne3
{ 59.Kg3 ?  loses the pawn endgame, since White is unable to answer ...Kxc5 with Kc3. }
5... h2 6. Nf5 h1=Q 7. Ng3+ { See Ending 15 & Ending 80. } *

