{ White played 56. Kd5. Was this a good idea ? } 1. Kd5
{ 56. Kd5 ? was a poor idea. The trick White relied on in the game, and which eventually turned out well for him, has in fact a major flaw, as the White king voluntarily abandons the winning zone.   56.Qh1  , among other moves, was easily winning, because the white king is in the winning zone  (two steps away from g3, to be precise)  . There might follow:  Ke2  57.Qxh2  Kf3   57...Ke1  58.Ke4  +-  as in the game  58.Qh1+  Ke2  59.Qe4+  Kd2  60.Qf3  Ke1  61.Qe3+  Kf1  62.Kf4  Kg2  63.Qe2  Kg1  64.Kg3  and checkmate. }
1... h1=Q+ 2. Qxh1 Ke2 3. Qh2
{ A tricky position. Whether or not the player with the Black pieces trusted his position, had he been familiar with - or remembered - the theoretical endgame, he wouldn't have had any problems finding }
3... Kf3
{ and Black draws.   58...Ke1 ??  Another one bites the dust.  59.Ke4 !  Ke2  60.Qg2  1-0.   See Ending 18 & Ending 19. }
