{ How should Black take the rook ? } 1... Kxc4
{ This is correct, but it is necessary to correctly assess the queen vs. two pawns endgame.   44...bxc4 ?  Capturing with the pawn is misleading: while it looks as if the pawn will promote, White's king can in fact return in time to capture it, and once two new queens appear on the board, the White king will be dangerously close.  45.Kxf6  Now a series of forced moves follows.  Kd3  46.Ke5  Kc2  47.Kd4  Kxb2  48.Kxc4  Kxa3  49.g4  Kb2 ?!   49...a4  would at least narrow down White's winning options to only one.  50.g5  Also winning is   50.Kb5  Kb3  51.g5 !  and the pawn promotes with check.  50...a4  51.g6  a3  52.g7  a2  53.g8=Q  a1=Q  Both sides have a new queen, but White wins quickly as he is able to give a horizontal check on the second rank.  54.Qg2+ !  Ka3   54...Kb1  55.Kb3  +-   54...Kc1  55.Qf1+  Kb2  56.Qe2+  reaches the game position.  55.Qf3+  Kb2  56.Qe2+  Ka3  57.Qe3+  Kb2  58.Qd2+  1-0. }
2. Kxf6 Kb3 3. g4 Kxb2 4. g5 a4 5. g6 b4 6. g7 bxa3 7. g8=Q a2
{ Because of the a4-pawn, the queen can't give check on b3. Furthermore, it's impossible to imprison the king, e.g. }
8. Qb8+ Kc2 9. Qc7+ Kb2 10. Qb6+ Kc2 11. Qc5+ Kb2 12. Qb4+ Kc2 13. Qc4+ Kb2
{ See Ending 20. } *

