[SetUp "1"]

{ Can Black win the game ? } 1... Qd5
{ Of course Black can win: he is a whole queen up ! Several roads lead to Rome, but alas, materialism can cloud a player's judgement. This move is easiest, pinning the c-pawn.   50...Qxb2+ ??  51.Kc8 ! =  Queen vs. pawn on the sixth is not always winning; particularly if the queen has no checks at her disposal, or, as is the case here, if the only check available doesn't help the attacker's cause.  Kf3  52.c7  Now it is queen vs. pawn on the seventh, and the result is a draw.  Ke4  53.Kd7  Qg7+  54.Kd8 ?!   54.Kc6 !  immediately heading for the queenside, would be easier.  54...Qf6+  55.Kd7  Qd4+  Interestingly, White has only one move to draw. This is a different version of the trick seen above.  56.Kc8 ?  A rather pessimistic choice. White must have been aware that the natural   56.Ke7 ?  is losing on account of  Qa7  57.Kd8  Kd5  58.c8=Q  Kd6  and mate is inevitable,  but   56.Ke8 !  is good enough to hold the draw.  56...Qg7 ?  A series of astonishing blunders, possibly prompted by extreme time trouble.   56...Qa7 !  forces checkmate as given in the variation above.  57.Kd8  Kd5  58.c8=N ??  A ludicrous finish !   58.c8=Q  Kd6  is not mate because the queen has two checks.  58...Qg8+  0-1.   50...Qb3+  also suffices, for example:  51.Kc8  Kf3  52.c7  Ke4  53.Kd7  Qd5+  54.Ke7  Qc6  55.Kd8  Qd6+  56.Kc8  Kd5  -+ }
2. Kb6 Kf3 3. c7 Qa8 { See Ending 17 & Ending 19. } *

