{ Can White draw the game ? } 1. Kf5
{ A mutual king race won't do: if White wants to save the game, he must meet his counterpart head on.  The natural   67.Kg5  starts a king race, but is no good:  Kb3 !  68.f5  Kc4  69.f6  Kd5  70.f7  Rb8  71.Kf6  Kd6  72.Kg7  Ke7  -+ }
1... Kb3 2. Ke5 Kc4 3. f5 Rb5+
{ 69...Kc5 !?  is perhaps a better practical choice, as after  70.f6  Kc6   Black is close to reaching a version of the standard  diagram A position, but unfortunately the poor placement of the rook doesn't do him any favours:  71.f7  Rb8  72.Ke6 = }
4. Ke6 Rb6+ 5. Ke5
{ The White king must keep blocking his enemy.   71.Ke7  Kd5  72.f6  Rb7+  -+ }
5... Rb5+ 6. Ke6 Kd4 { Opting to exert some pressure from behind. } 7. f6 Rb6+
8. Ke7 Ke5 9. f7 Rb7+ 10. Ke8 Ke6
{ Some tournament players still believe this position is winning. You have to love seeing their faces when suddenly a knight appears on the board. }
11. f8=N+ { And drawn, thirty moves later.  See Ending 21 & Ending 24. } *

