{ Can Black win the game ? } 1... Rf4+
{ It was necessary to give a check at once to avoid the well-known, though in this position unusual drawing resource we'll see below.  Black can win, but he has to be careful; as we are about to see, the rook on Black's fifth rank is badly placed.   54...Kd6 ?  55.g6   This is almost  our familiar standard diagram position, but the Black rook is too close, allowing a surprising defensive resource:  Rf4+  56.Kg5 !  Rf1  57.g7  The pawn is on the seventh, but it doesn't really threaten to promote.  Ke7  The king comes closer, forcing an unusual knight promotion.  58.g8=N+ !  We know this idea, but usually the king is on the edge of the board. It's still a draw, though.  Ke6  59.Nh6  Ke5  60.Ng4+  Ke4  61.Nf6+  Kd4  62.Ng4  And a draw was agreed after nineteen more moves. }
2. Ke5 Rg4 3. Kf6 Kd6 4. g6
{ and now that the rook is already behind the pawn, the Black king can get in: }
4... Kd7 { 57...Rf4+ ?  58.Kg5 !  as in the game. } 5. g7 Ke8 *

