{ What is the result after the following moves:  (a)  53... g5; and  (b)  53... Kxf3 ? }
1... Kxf3
{ Both moves draw, but one of them requires finding a difficult second move. This one is easiest:   53...g5 ?!  54.Rf7 !  Kxf3 ?  The losing error. It was necessary to bring the king to the right side, to hinder its colleague.     54...Ke5 !  55.Kc6  g4  56.fxg4  fxg4  57.Kc5  Ke4 !  58.Kc4  g3 !  59.Rg7  Kf3  60.Kd3 =  Reaching once again a version of  diagram A from the introduction.  55.Rxf5+  Kg4  Going backwards is always undesirable, but the worst thing is that the king now has to move to the wrong side of the pawn and will get mated.  56.Rf8  Kh3  Black needs four tempi to escort the pawn home, while White needs six tempi to forestall this. White has a trump, though: Black's poorly placed king is a serious liability.  57.Kc6  g4  58.Kd5  g3  59.Ke4  g2  60.Kf3   All symmetrical options included, this position has occurred six times according to my database: it compels Black to go for knight promotion as seen in  diagram G from the introduction, which loses here.  Kh2  This is one of two games that did not see a knight appear on the board, but the result is the same.   60...g1=N+  Loses quickly.  61.Kf2  Kh2  62.Rh8+  Nh3+  63.Kf3  -+  61.Rh8+  1-0. }
2. Rxg6 f4 3. Kc6 Ke2 4. Re6+ Kd2 5. Rf6 Ke3 6. Kc5 f3
{ and the White king is too slow. } *

