{ Black to move will soon win a rook. Can he win the game ? } 1... Rf4
{ Black wins, but he shouldn't be too hasty and go after the rook immediately. Instead, he should cut the enemy king off first.   56...Ke2 ?  This move unnecessarily complicates matters. The king had to be cut off on the fourth rank.  57.Rh1 ?  Neither player seems to be aware of the concept of cutting off the king.   57.Rxf2+  is enough for a draw:  Kxf2  58.Kc4 !   58.a5 ?  Rf4  -+   58.Kb4 ?  Ke3 !  59.a5  Kd4  60.Kb5  Rf5+ !  61.Kb4  Rf1  62.Kb5  Kd5  63.Kb6  Kd6  64.a6  Rb1+  -+  58...Rf5  59.Kb4 !  (it may seem odd, but now the black rook is worse)  Ke3  60.a5  Kd4  61.a6 =  57...f1=Q ?   57...Rf4 !  was still winning.  58.Rxf1  Rxf1  59.a5 ?   59.Kc4 !  Not letting his own king get cut off while hindering Black's, was again sufficient to hold a draw.  59...Kd3   59...Rf4 !  cutting off the king, was still easiest. However, the move in the game is also winning.  60.Kb4  Kd4  61.Kb5  Kd5  62.Kb6  Kd6  63.a6   A version of  diagram A from the introduction has been reached again.  Rb1+ !  -+  64.Ka7  Kc6  65.Ka8  Kb6  66.a7  Rg1  0-1. }
2. a5 Ke2 3. Rxf2+ Kxf2 { See Ending 22. } *

