{ Can White win? } 1. Rc8
{ The move in the game, while winning, unnecessarily complicates matters, and sets the stage for the error three moves later.  The most technical, and therefore easiest, way to win is to first cut off the king with   54.Rc5 !  Ra7+  55.Kd8  Rxe7  56.Kxe7 }
1... Ra7+ 2. Kd6 Rxe7 3. Kxe7 Kf5 4. Rc5+
{ 57.Rh8 ??  An error revealing a lack of understanding of the basic ideas in this endgame: against a rook's pawn, the most effective method is to persecute the king from behind with your own king, combined with horizontal checks, knowing that knight promotion is no solution for the defender in such a scenario:  Kg4  Now, on the other hand, White lacks one tempo to carry out an effective horizontal king persecution.  58.Ke6   58.Kf6  h4  59.Ra8  h3  60.Ra4+  Kg3  61.Kg5  h2 =  58...h4  59.Ke5  h3  60.Ke4  Kg3  61.Ke3  The critical position has been reached with the side with the pawn to move, thus ensuring a draw.  Kg2 !  62.Rg8+  Kf1  63.Rh8  Kg2  64.Ke2  h2  65.Rg8+  Kh1 !  66.Kf2  Draw. }
4... Kg4 5. Kf6 h4 6. Rc4+ Kg3 7. Kg5 h3 8. Rc3+ Kg2 9. Kg4 h2 10. Rc2+ Kg1 11.
Kg3 { Here promoting to a knight can't save the game. } 11... h1=N+ 12. Kf3
{ The knight is lost.  See Ending 22, Ending 27 & Ending 28. } *

