{ Is sacrificing the knight enough to force a draw ? } 1. Nxc4
{ Giving up the knight is objectively losing for White. Given the result of the game, however, it's probably the most practical try: }
1... Rxc4 2. Kxf5 Kxb2
{ A typical error, losing a precious tempo by capturing an unimportant pawn. The king had to be brought closer at once:   63...Kd3  64.Ke5  Rc5+  65.Ke6  Ke4  is an easy win. }
3. Ke5
{ It is necessary to go to the other side and use shouldering.   64.Kg5 ??  This move allows the Black king to become involved.  Rc1 ??  Here, it was sufficient to bring the king closer:   64...Kc3 !  65.f5  Kd4  66.f6  Ke5  67.f7  Rf4  68.Kg6  Ke6  -+  65.f5  Rg1+  66.Kh6  Rf1  67.Kg6  Kc3  68.f6  Kd4  Draw. }
3... Kc3 4. f5 Rc5+ 5. Ke6 Kd4 6. f6 Rc6+ 7. Ke7 Ke5 8. f7 Rc7+ 9. Ke8 Ke6
{ And it's a theoretical draw after } 10. f8=N+ { See Ending 21 & Ending 23. }
