{ Does Black have time to eliminate the last remaining white pawn, and help his own h-pawn ? }
1... Kg6
{ Black has time to both win the g-pawn and help the h-pawn. In the game, however, the player with the Black pieces was not up to the job. The first couple of moves are forced for both sides: }
2. Rf8 Kxg7 3. Rxf2 Kg6 4. Kb7 Kg5 5. Kc6 Kg4 6. Kd5 h4 7. Ke4 h3
{ 73...Kg3  would lose:  74.Rf3+  Kg2  75.Kf4  h3  76.Rg3+  Kh2  77.Kf3  White wins. }
8. Ke3 Kg3
{ White would normally be winning, but in this position he can't: the rook is on an unfavourable square, unable to give a safe check. }
9. Rf8
{ Reaching the most important position in this endgame. We know that the side with the pawn can make a draw if it's their turn to move. However, the position is not entirely trivial, and it's not too late to botch it. }
9... Kg2
{ is the way to draw.   75...h2 ??  A blunder, aiming to promote to a knight in the corner, by which Black shows his lack of understanding of the endgame; now he is lost.  76.Rg8+  Kh3  77.Kf2 !  h1=N+  78.Kf3  Kh2  79.Rg7  1-0.   See Ending 28. }
