{ What should Black play ? } 1... Rxa6
{ The best thing to do is to allow b7-b8=Q, as we shall see.   68...Rxb7+ ?  The wrong choice.  69.Kxb7  Kg4  The best try, trying to contest the enemy king, but it should not suffice.   69...g4 ?!  is much less resistant:  70.Kc6  g3  71.Kd5  g2  72.Ra1  Kg4  73.Ke4  +-  .  70.Kc6 ?  The winning move is   70.Rf6 !!  Confining the king to the g- and h-files: a common theme.  Kh3  71.Kc6  g4  72.Kd5  g3  73.Ke4  g2  74.Kf3  (Forcing a position in which promotion to a knight won't save Black.)  g1=N+  75.Kf2  Kh2  76.Rh6+  Nh3+  77.Kf3  +-  70...Kf3 !  Completing the body check.  71.Kd5  g4  72.Rf6+  The rook moves behind the pawn with tempo, but it's not good enough.  Ke3  73.Rg6  Kf3  74.Kd4  g3  75.Kd3   Reaching a version of  diagram A from the introduction for the umpteenth time.  g2 =  76.Kd2  Kf2  77.Rf6+  Kg3  78.Rg6+  Kf2  79.Rxg2+  Kxg2  Draw. }
2. b8=Q Rf6
{ This would lead to a theoretically drawn position, albeit one in which Black would have to suffer for some time.  See Ending 26 & Ending 99. }
*

