[SetUp "1"]

{ What should Black play after Ra1? } 1. Ra1
{ White can win, but not with the move played in the game. This position might resemble the previous one, but in this case there is a hidden stalemate motif. Therefore, ...f4-f3 must not be allowed.   63.Ra3 !  preventing  f3+  wins easily. }
1... f3+ 2. Ke3 f2 3. Ke2
{ This position is usually winning against central pawns. However, against pawn duos on the a- and b-files, b- and c-files, f- and g-files, or g-and h-files, it's a draw due to stalemate motifs. }
3... Kh2 4. Kf1 Kh1 5. Rb1 Kh2 6. Ke2 Kg2 7. Ra1 Kh2 8. Kf3 f1=Q+
{ This is the defensive idea: Black gives up both pawns to force stalemate. }
9. Rxf1 g2 10. Rf2 Kh1 11. Ke2
{ A good sense of humour. White prefers drawing against a material superiority.   73.Rxg2  Stalemate, was the natural finish. }
11... g1=Q 12. Rf1 Qxf1+ 13. Kxf1 { Draw.  See Ending 31. } *

