{ How should Black defend? } 1. h6
{ Despite the offside Black king, White can't win, unless the Black rook is unable to give any checks from behind. The main rule that applies to this kind of endgame is as follows: it's a draw if the rook is on the first or the second rank; if it's on any other rank, the side with the rook loses. The exact placement of the Black king is, perhaps surprisingly, never an issue: it ensures a draw from any square on the board. Notwithstanding these theoretical generalities, according to my database, this concrete position, including all possible symmetries and positions with the rook on different squares, has occurred in only a few games. Other moves don't win either:   65.g5  Kb3  66.g6  Rg1 ! =   65.Kg5  Kb3  66.Kf6  Kc4  67.g5  Kd5  68.g6  Rf1+  69.Ke7  Re1+  70.Kf7  Rf1+  71.Kg8  Rh1 = }
1... Rh1+
{ Another game in my database took a different course, which was also correct:   65...Kb3  66.g5  Rh1+  67.Kg4  Kc4  68.Kf5  Kd5  69.Kf6  Ke4  70.Kg7  Kf5  Draw, Martinez de Negri-Spata, Argentina tt 2016. }
2. Kg5 Kb3 3. Kg6 Kc4 4. g5 Kd5 5. Kg7 { 69.Kh7  Ke6  70.g6  Rg1 ! = } 5... Ke6
6. g6 Rh2 7. h7 Kf5
{ Draw. The king latches onto the hindmost pawn: tag you're it !  See Ending 32. }
*

