{ Can Black save the game ? } 1... Ra3
{ One of two correct moves. Black is preparing to build a bridge, thus forcing White to sacrifice his rook for the pawn, after which the enemy king will be one square closer:   70...e1=Q ??  Black can save the game. This, however, can't be achieved by immediately queening the pawn, which would allow White to queen one of his own, thanks to the technique of switching pole position. With this move, Black's king will be dragged away too far from the action.  71.Rxe1  Kxe1  72.Kb6  Kd2  The crucial moment: the rook controls the furthermost pawn, so White, by switching pole position, decides to support the other one.  73.Ka7 !  1-0. This is what Black had missed: the hindmost pawn that White suddenly turns his attention to, is out of the Black king's reach. In conclusion, we can establish the following rule: the defending king, to make a draw, mustn't be more than one step away from the enemy pawn, i.e. in this particular position it should have been on the d3-square, for example.  The second correct move,   70...Kd3  Seems strange, but is equally effective because the king approaches the White pawns and if White puts his king on b6, Black takes on e1 with his rook.  71.Kb6   71.Rxe2  Kxe2  draws because the Black king is one square closer  71...e1=Q  72.Rxe1  Rxe1  73.Ka7  Re7+  74.Kb8  Kc4  75.b6  Kb5 = }
2. Rxe2+ { 71.Kb6  Re3  72.Rxe3  Kxe3  73.a7  e1=Q  74.a8=Q = } 2... Kxe2 3.
Kb6 { 72.b6  Ra5+ ! } 3... Kd3 4. Ka7 Kc4 5. b6 Kb5
{ and the king, having saved a tempo, plays tag: } 6. b7 Rxa6+ 7. Kb8
{ See Ending 32. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook vs. Two Pawns"]
[Black "Igor Glek vs. Smbat Lputian"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/1PK5/2P2k2/5P2/8/8/1r6 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Can Black save the game ? } 1... Ke4
{ A familiar sight: two connected pawns supported by their king vis-à-vis a defending rook placed behind them. Bearing this precedent in mind  (see previous exercise)  , you might sense what White is up to: a switch in pole position. Black's king, therefore, had better hurry up.  Black has no time to indulge in the pawn: after   67...Kxf4 ?  the king is too far from the pawn, and there follows a switch in pole position:  68.Kb7 !  Ke5  69.c6  Kd6  70.c7  +-   67...Ke6 ?  prevents the switch of pole position, but loses nonetheless on account of  68.b7  and Black's king can't play tag with the c-pawn, because the f-pawn controls the e5-square. }
2. f5 { Intending to divert the White king.   68.Kb7  Kd5  69.c6  Kc5 ! = }
2... Kxf5
{ Now that the Black king is close enough to the d7-square, it can capture the pawn.   68...Ke5  would also make a draw. }
3. Kb7 { 69.b7  Ke5  70.Kc7  Kd5 = } 3... Ke6 4. c6 Rc1 5. Kc7 { 71.c7  Kd7 = }
5... Kd5 6. b7 { Draw.  See Ending 32. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook vs. Two Pawns"]
[Black "Gata Kamsky vs. Vasil Spasov"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/5K2/5PP1/8/1k6/8/r7 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Choose between 56... Rg1, 56... Ra6 and 56... Kc4. } 1... Kc4
{ Any logical king move is good enough to draw: Black simply intends to play tag.   56...Ra6+ ?  This move, as well as any other rook move, leads to a lost position.  57.Ke5 ?!  This move, although it doesn't throw away the win, complicates matters.   57.Kg7 !  makes way for the f-pawn, while not allowing Black to latch onto the other pawn:  Kc4  58.f6  Kd5  59.f7  Ra8  60.f8=Q  Rxf8  61.Kxf8  Ke6  62.g6  +-  57...Ra5+  58.Ke6  Ra6+  59.Ke5 ?  Accepting a draw in a winning position.   59.Kf7 !  was still winning, for example:  Kc4  60.g6  Kd5  61.g7  Ra8  62.f6  Ke5  and now Black effectively seems to be saying 'tag, you're it', but the problem is the position of his rook; there follows 63. Kg6 and White wins. This example shows that for a successful game of tag, the rook must be behind the pawns.    56...Rg1 ?  is not enough either, because after  57.g6  Kc4  White switches pole position with  58.Kg7 ! }
2. g6 Kd5 3. g7 Rg1 4. Kf7 Ke5 { Keep sticking to the pawn. } 5. f6 Kf5
{ See Ending 32. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook vs. Two Pawns"]
[Black "Valery Shalimov vs. Vladimir Onischuk"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r3R3/1K6/8/3p4/8/3kp3/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ How should White capture the rook ? } 1. Rxa8
{ Capturing with the rook is the more flexible choice, as the king will have to go back to b7 anyway, while the rook can still choose which pawn to aim at from behind.   58.Kxa8 ?  loses:  d4  59.Kb7  Now Black can choose whether to promote the d- or the e-pawn:  Ke2 !  Switching pole position; White might have missed this idea.   59...e2 ?  is the obvious variation, but is incorrect:  60.Kc6  Kd2  61.Kd5  d3  62.Kd4 =  Tag, you're it!  60.Kc6  d3  61.Kc5  d2  62.Rd8  d1=Q  -+ }
1... d4 2. Kc6 Kc3
{ 59...e2  60.Re8 =  Transposes to the variation 58. Kxa8 above.   59...Ke2  Switching pole position, is now harmless, as the White king will soon join in:  60.Kd5  d3  61.Ke4  d2  62.Rd8 = }
3. Re8 { Other moves also draw, but this is the most natural. } 3... Kd2 4. Kd5
d3 5. Kd4 e2 6. Re7 e1=Q 7. Rxe1 { Draw.  See Ending 32. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook vs. Two Pawns"]
[Black "Boris Shatskes vs. Grigory Goldberg"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/pR6/2P4r/kPK5/8/6pp/P7/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ What result can Black hope for ? } 1... a6
{ Objectively speaking, the position is a draw, but Black has one move that almost wins by force. In fact, this is exactly what happened in the game.  After   59...Rh5+  making a draw is easy for both sides:  60.Kc4  a6  61.Ra7  Rh4+  62.Kc5  Rh5+ =  The direct   59...Rxc6+ ?  even loses:  60.bxc6  a6  61.Rb3 !  This gain of tempo is decisive. }
2. Ra7 Rxc6+
{ Black can give up the rook, as his pawn duo has become an unstoppable force. }
3. Kxc6 g2 4. a3
{ White again threatens checkmate, but he should have settled for   62.Rxa6+ !  Kb4  63.b6 !  g1=Q  64.b7 =  Despite his material advantage and the pawn on Black's sixth rank, he can't win:  Qg6+  65.Kc7  Qf7+  66.Kc8  Qf8+  67.Kc7  h2  68.Rh6 !  Qxh6  69.b8=Q+  and this endgame is a draw. }
4... Ka4 5. Rxa6+ Kb3 6. b6 g1=Q 7. b7 { Now Black is winning. } 7... Qg6+ 8.
Kc7 Qf7+ 9. Kc8 Qf8+ 10. Kc7 h2 11. Rh6 Qg7+ 12. Kc8 Qxh6 13. b8=Q+ Ka2
{ This is the difference compared to the variation after move 62. Black's king hides from the checks. }
14. Qe5 Qc1+ { 0-1. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook vs. Two Pawns"]
[Black "Michael Roiz vs. Zvulon Gofshtein"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/k7/5pP1/5P1N/1r6/5K2/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ How should White react after 78...Rb8? } 1... Rb8
{ At first glance, there doesn't seem to be any difference between two rook moves, since in either case, the White king must defend the pawn, allowing his counterpart to come closer. But this move loses. As we're about to see, White prefers to keep his pawn rather than his knight.   78...Rb4 !  The correct move.  79.Ke3  Kb5 !  80.Nxf5  Kc5 !  81.g6  Rb6  82.g7  Rg6 = }
2. Nxf5
{ 79.Ke3 ?  This would lead to a similar variation analysed above.  Kb6 !  80.Nxf5  Kc7  81.Nh6  Rb6  82.f5  Kd6  83.g6  Ke5 ! }
2... Rf8 { 79...Kb7  doesn't help either:  80.g6  Kc7  81.Nh6  +- } 3. Kg3
{ This is the move that Black missed. In his legendary work My system, Nimzowitsch showed an almost identical position to illustrate the concept of the blockade.   80.g6 ?  is another fairly logical try, but after  Rf6 !  81.g7  Rg6  White can't make any significant progress before the Black king gets involved. }
3... Rxf5 4. Kg4 Rf8 5. g6 Kb7
{ Again, the Black king, while apparently so far away, is actually only one tempo short. }
6. f5 Kc6 7. g7 Rg8 8. f6 Kd6 { 85...Kd7  86.Kf5  Ke8  87.Ke6 } 9. Kf5 { 1-0. }
*

