1. Bg2
{ This is the only square on the long diagonal that loses. This is a position of mutual zugzwang. The White bishop must be on g2 when its counterpart moves to d3. We shall soon see why.   65.Bb7 !  Bd3  66.Bg2 !  Be4  67.Kf1 =   65.Ba8  and   65.Bh1  also draw. }
1... Bd3
{ Zugzwang. The point is that if the bishop stays on the long diagonal, Black plays ...Be4, forcing a winning king-and-pawn endgame. But if the bishop goes to h3, the Black king manages to infiltrate to h2, after which it will become apparent that the bishop is on a critically short diagonal, and therefore the game is lost. }
2. Bh3 Kf3 3. Bf1 Bf5 4. Bb5 Kg2 5. Bc6+ Kg1 6. Ke2 Bh3 7. Kf3 Kh2 8. Ke3 Bg2
9. Bb5 Bd5 10. Bf1 Bc4 11. Bxc4 g2 12. Kd2 g1=Q 13. Kc3 Kg3
{ 0-1.  See Ending 35. } *

