{ Can Black save the game ? } 1... Bd8
{ Black can just about save the game, provided he makes all the most accurate moves. }
2. Bd6
{ The immediate   65.Bc7  doesn't make much sense as it doesn't win the a-pawn, and after  Bg5  66.Bd6  Black would have two defensive possibilities at his disposal, although the basic defensive set-up with  Ke4 !  would still be good. }
2... a5
{ The pawn provides an all-important gain of tempo. In the variation   65...Bh4 ?  66.Be7  Be1  67.Bd8  Bb4  68.Bc7  the Black king has no time to reach the d5-square. }
3. Bc7 { 66.Be7  Bxe7  67.Kxe7  a4  and both pawns queen. } 3... Bg5 4. Bxa5
Ke5
{ Karpov accepted the draw here, as the Black king reaches the correct defensive set-up. Engine-addicts may be delighted to see that there is another move to draw the game, afforded by the extra tempo. However, the manoeuvre itself is unique, as after, say,   67...Bh4  68.Bb4  the move  Ke4 !  already becomes imperative:  69.Be7  Be1  70.Bd8  Bb4  71.Bc7  Kd5 !   See Ending 34. }
