{ Can White save the game ? } 1. Kf1
{ The king goes to g1, from where it cannot be evicted, and awaits the exchange of bishops on g4 to reach a drawn pawn endgame. This defensive method is generally poorly understood.   83.Bd1 ?  White can save the game, but not by taking the opposition from behind, as seen in the previous exercises. This time, frontal defence is called for.  Be6 ?  Black, being handed a golden opportunity, shows he doesn't understand the requirements of the position either:   83...Be4 !  (other moves also win)  84.Kf1  Bf3 !  85.Bc2  Bg2+  -+  followed by ...f4-f3.  84.Kf1 !  White finally discovers the defensive mechanism, and holds onto it for the remainder of the game.  Bd5  85.Kg1  Bc6  86.Be2  Ba8  87.Bd1  Bf3  88.Bb3  But not   88.Bxf3 ??  Kxf3  -+  88...Bg4  89.Bd5  Bh5  90.Bc6  Be2  91.Bd5  Bd1  92.Bc6  Bf3  93.Bb5  Bg2  94.Be2  f3  95.Bxf3  Draw.   See Ending 36. }
