{ Is it possible to calculate whether this is a winning or a drawn position ? }
1... Ke6
{ This position is winning, and Stohl started off on the right foot: offering an exchange of bishops is more important than bringing the king closer.   65...Ke4 ?  66.Kf2  Kd3  67.Bd6  Kc2  68.Ke2  Bc3  69.Be7  Kb3  70.Kd1  and exchanging the bishops is now never going to bring any dividends:  Bb4  71.Bxb4  Kxb4  72.Kc2 =  . }
2. Kf3 { The simple plan } 2... Be7
{ 66...Kd5 ??  Draw. Oddly, Black changes plans and decides to offer an immediate draw, while he could play on for a win at no risk, something he could have attained with perfect play.  67.Ke3  Kc4  68.Bf8  Kb3  leads nowhere. }
3. Bc3 a3
{ followed by ...Bf6 wins the game. The most significant factor is not so much any particular motif in bishop endgames, but rather the basic rule of the square of the pawn: the king is outside ! }
