{ Can White hold this position ? } 1. Kc1
{ White draws by using a frontal defence. The point of this concept is twofold: the king cannot be evicted from the b1-square, and an exchange of bishops on the b4-square leads to a drawn pawn endgame. Judging by the way the game developed, it's clear that neither player knew about these ideas.   67.Bd2 ?  The White king is unable to take up the correct defensive stand from behind the pawn. However, another defensive resource is available: frontal defence.  Bf2 ?   67...Bd4  Among other moves, would thwart White's intentions to take up the defensive stand:  68.Kc1  Bc3 !  69.Bf4  Bb2+ !  70.Kb1  c3  -+  .  68.Ba5 ?  Obviously, the correct move was   68.Kc1 ! =  68...Bg3 ?   68...Bd4 !  -+  .  69.Bd2 ?   69.Kc1 !  69...Be5 !  70.Be1  Bc3 !  Two correct moves in a row by Black.  71.Bg3  And in this position, for some reason a draw was agreed. Black is still winning with the typical manoeuvre   71.Bg3  Bb4  72.Be5  Ba3  followed by 73... Bb2.   See Ending 36. }
*

