{ Here, too, Black's king is far away from the action. Is White winning ? } 1.
Kf5
{ White wins, since the bishop is forced either to go onto a three-square diagonal, from which it can be expelled by a joint effort of White's pieces, or to occupy the long diagonal, where it can be obstructed. }
1... Be3 2. g6 Bh6 3. Ke6 Bg7
{ If Black waits for Kf7 and later Ng4, he will have to discharge the bishop from its duty to control g7. }
4. Kf7 Bh8 5. Nh5
{ 1-0. The square occupied by the knight  (h5 or any symmetrical equivalents)  is the key square with an a-, b- or g-pawn on the sixth rank. White threatens 57. Ng7 followed by 58. Kg8, and if the bishop moves away, the knight will interfere on f6. In such situations, the only way Black can draw is by attacking the pawn with the king, a different version of playing tag  (see Chapter 5)  , when White plays Ng7 and Kg8. To be able to do this successfully, the king needs to be three steps away from f6 or g5, to be able to meet the manoeuvre Ng7 and Kg8 with ...Kf6 or ... Kg5. More distant drawing squares are numbered 1. Beyond those, the Black king is helpless. }
