{ Is 69... Kg1 winning for Black ? } 1... Ne3
{ Many players falsely believe this endgame is always a draw, while others don't even know the main defensive idea. The truth is, however, that under normal circumstances Black wins, as long as he doesn't attack the bishop prematurely.   69...Kg1 ?  A premature attack on the bishop in the corner ! This move shouldn't be played before making sure that the defending king is unable to imprison the attacker's. It's much better to force the defending king to find some very accurate moves, although in this position most logical moves lead to a win.  70.Ke2 !  A premature attack on the bishop eases the defence considerably: White waits for Black to take the bishop, and then plays Kf1 or Kf2, whichever is the same colour as the square the enemy knight is on. We shall soon see why.  Nf2  If Black captures now, White must play the king to a light square:   70...Kxh1  71.Kf1 ! =  71.Bb7  Nh3  72.Bh1  Nf4+  73.Ke1  Kxh1  74.Kf2 !  Draw. Now the king moves to a dark square. This way, the knight can't dislodge it.   74.Kf1 ??  would be a monumental blunder, as  Nd3  would win on the spot. }
2. Kd2 Ng2 3. Kd3 Nh4 4. Kd2 Kf1 5. Kd1 Nf5 6. Kd2 Kg1 7. Ke1 Nh4 8. Ke2 Ng2
{ Zugzwang.  See Ending 38. } *

