{ White could have saved the game with a neat defence. With the first move he starts off on the right foot. }
1... Kh2
{ Black wants to get to the pawn by bringing his king all the way around. This succeeds only thanks to White's kind cooperation. Insufficient is   65...Kg2 ?!  66.Nf4+  with an immediate draw.  The same goes for   65...Bc7 !?  (controlling the f4-square)  66.Kd2  Kf1  67.Ke3 = }
2. Ne1
{ This move exploits the fact that the Black king is for the moment slightly offside:   66.Kb1 ?  This looks natural. Could there be anything else ? Yes, there is !  Kg3  67.Kc2  Kf3  68.Ne5+   68.Kb3  Ke3  69.Kc2  Kd4  -+  Zugzwang.  68...Ke4  69.Nd3  Ke3  0-1. }
2... Kg3 3. Kd3 Kf2 4. Nc2
{ and the knight establishes a stable position to control the pawn on the seventh.  See Ending 40. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Bishop vs. Knight: One Pawn on the Board"]
[Black "Klaudia Kulon vs. Roman Staruch"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/4k3/2b5/2N1p1K1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Choose between 80... Bd4 and 80... Kd3. } 1... Kd3
{ Black is winning, but not any move will do. The main idea is to force zugzwang. The procedure explained in Ending 39 doesn't work, however, since the square on the seventh rank  (a light square)  is of a different colour than the  (dark-squared)  bishop. It's necessary to pre-empt effective coordination between the enemy king and knight.   80...Bd4 ?  81.Ne1  Ke3  82.Nc2+   82.Nf3  was not an option:  Bf6 !  and the same zugzwang position we saw above is impending  (see also the comment after move 88)  .  82...Kd3  Now, however, Black can't take away the blockading square from the knight with   82...Kd2  because of  83.Nxd4.  83.Ne1+  Kd2  84.Nf3+  Kd1  85.Kg3  Be3  86.Kg2  Kc2  87.Kg3  Kd3  Until now, White has defended adequately to maintain the equilibrium in an interesting position, but he seems uncertain about the correct defensive set-up, and, unfortunately for him, now decides to change the position of his king:  88.Kg2 ?  White should only move the king when the bishop is on e1. Meanwhile, the knight must hop back and forth between e1 and f3.   88.Ne1+  Ke4  89.Nf3 =  88...Ke4 ?  Black fails to seize the opportunity.   88...Bb6 !  89.Kg3   89.Ne1+  amounts to the same  89...Ke3  90.Ne1  Bc7+  91.Kg2  Be5  92.Kg1  Bg3  93.Nc2+  Kd2  -+  89.Kg3  Bb6  90.Kg2 ?  White, as so often occurs, offers a draw in a lost position. Who can blame him ? Moreover, it appears to have been accepted. }
2. Kf3 Kd2 3. Kf2 { Black should now aim for zugzwang with } 3... Bf6 4. Kf3
Bh4 { See Ending 40. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Bishop vs. Knight: One Pawn on the Board"]
[Black "Darryl Johansen vs. Ian Rogers"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/6B1/8/8/8/1n6/p1k2K2/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Can Black win the game ? } 1... Nc5
{ Black wins by a rather difficult manoeuvre. The point is to be careful not to let the king be imprisoned once it captures the bishop on a1. The knight is most flexible on the c5-square; the manoeuvre now depends on what route the White king chooses. }
2. Ke3
{ This square is too far away and makes things easier for Black. The stubbornest is   58.Ke2  when Black must lose a tempo with the king:  Kc1 !  59.Ke3   59.Ke1  Nd3+  followed by 60... Nb2   59.Ba1  Kb1  60.Kd1  Na4  61.Kd2  Nb2  -+  Reaching the zugzwang position.  59...Na4  60.Ba1  Kb1  61.Kd2  Nb2  -+  Reaching the same zugzwang position as in the main variation anyway. }
2... Na4 { Threatens 59... Nb2. } 3. Ba1 Kb1 4. Kd2
{ 60.Kd3  Nc5+  and the king can't go to d2. } 4... Nb2
{ This model zugzwang position is worth remembering: White can't imprison the Black king. }
5. Kc3 Kxa1 6. Kc2 Nd3 { White resigned.  See Ending 38. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Bishop vs. Knight: One Pawn on the Board"]
[Black "Zorica Puljek Salai vs. Ildiko Madl"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/8/1K1knp2/5B2/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Is any of the following logical moves winning: 63... Ng4, 63... Nd1 or 63... Ke2 ? }
1... Ng4
{ None of the suggested moves is winning against correct defence, i.e. by keeping the bishop on its longest diagonal. In the game, however, Black won quickly:   63...Nd1  leaves White with the same choice:  64.Bg1 !  if   64.Bg3 ?  Ke2  controls half the squares on the bishop's new diagonal, while the knight has time to take away the other half from the f5-square:  65.Kc4  Ne3+  66.Kc5  Nf5  and the bishop is expelled  64...Ke2  65.Kc4  Nf2  66.Bh2  Ng4  67.Bg1  Kf1  68.Bb6  Ke2  69.Bg1 ! =   63...Ke2  64.Bg1 ! =  leads to similar variations. }
2. Bg1
{ 64.Bg3 ?  No sooner does the bishop voluntarily - and unwisely - occupy the shorter  (four squares long)  diagonal, than Black is motivated to arrange his pieces aimed at total control of it. White had to stay on the longer diagonal.  Ke2  Black's king rules over the e1- and f2-squares.  65.Kb2  Ne3  66.Kc1  Nf5 !  0-1. And the knight now conquers the g3- and h4-squares. }
2... Ke2 3. Kc4 Nf2 { The only way to get the bishop off the longer diagonal. }
4. Kd5 Kf1 5. Bh2 Kg2 6. Bc7
{ Care is required, as a move such as   68.Bd6 ?  loses to  Nd3 !  and the bishop can't get back on the diagonal. }
6... Nd1 7. Bb6
{ and the bishop is back on its longest diagonal.   See Ending 37. } *

