[White "Bishop vs. Knight: One Pawn on the Board"]
[Black "Martin Cerny vs. Bohuslav Dubansky"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/4k3/1PK5/n7/8/7B/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Can White win the game ? } 1. Kb5
{ Despite the pawn being only on the sixth rank, Black has no way to construct a stable blockade at any time, because of the lack of coordination between his king and knight.   89.Kc7 ?  This, however, is not the right move: the king should be harassing the knight. Now, Black has all the time in the world for such ugly and otherwise losing ideas of trying to get the king round all the way via f6.  Kf6  90.Bd7  Ke5 !  Black finds the right plan, consisting of bringing the king close to the knight as well as to the enemy pawn. Had he stayed passive, White would have been able to get in Kb8-a7-a6, disturbing the knight. Specifically,   90...Ke7 ?  loses on account of  91.Bb5  Ke6  92.Kb8 !  This is the winning manoeuvre.  Kd6  93.Ka7  Kc5  94.Bd7  Kb4  95.Be8  Kc5  96.Ka6  Kb4  97.Bf7  Ka4  98.Bg6  Kb4  99.Bc2  Zugzwang.  Nc6  100.b7  Kc5  101.Ba4  Nb8+  102.Ka7  +-  91.Bc6  Kd4  92.Be8  Kc5  93.Bf7  Kb5  94.Be8+  Kc5  95.Bd7  Kd4  96.Bg4  Kd5  97.Bh5  Kd4  98.Be2  Kc5  Draw. }
1... Nb7 2. Bc8 Nd6+ 3. Kc5 Ne4+ 4. Kc6 Nd6 5. Ba6
{ What White has achieved is a position in which the knight has neither a stable blockade, nor any permanent control over the pawn. The next phase is about forcing zugzwang by means of some simple, patient manoeuvring: }
5... Ke6 6. Be2 Ke7 7. Bb5 Ke6 8. Ba4 Ke7 9. Bb3 { See Ending 40. } *

