{ Should Black play 38... g5 ? } 1... g5
{ Black should go for ...g6-g5, as he can easily reach the correct defensive set-up, i.e. the bishop attacking the pawn from in front. Other waiting moves also draw, but aren't as clear-cut. White could play g4-g5 and after f2-f4, Kg3-g4. From that position, Black should watch out for h4-h5 as well as Bxg6, even though, objectively speaking, neither of these moves should trouble Black. }
2. hxg5+ Kxg5 3. f4+
{ The white pawns are going to reach the fifth rank  (f5 and g5)  . Black should already be thinking about how to set up his king and bishop once they get there: the king belongs on g7 and the bishop on d8 or e7. See the position after move 48. }
3... Kf6 4. g4 Be3 5. Kf3 Bc1 6. g5+ Kg7 7. Kg4 Ba3 8. Be8 Bb4 9. Kf3 Bd2 10.
Kg4 Bb4
{ Simply the best. It's true that Black could wait and answer f4-f5 with Bc3; there's time to play the bishop to a different diagonal. But why complicate matters ? }
11. f5 Be7 { Draw. The basic defensive stand.  See Ending 42 } *

