{ Can White reach the basic defensive stand ? } 1. Kd2
{ White needs his king on f2 and the bishop on d2 to hold this position. Unfortunately for him, his bishop is poorly placed. Provided Black is careful and doesn't open the door for the bishop by means of ...e5-e4, White will need six tempi to reach the defensive set-up, a luxury he can't afford. }
1... f4 2. Bg7 Kf5 3. Ke1 Bb5 4. Kf2
{ 66.Bf8  is no good either in view of  e4  67.Bb4  e3  -+  . } 4... Bc6 5. Bf8
e4 6. Bc5
{ Preventing ...e4-e3 for the time being, so Black must set the winning plan in motion. }
6... Ke5 7. Ba7 Be8 8. Bb6 Bh5
{ 70...Ke6 ?!  Black fails to complete the preparatory stage.  71.Ba7  Curiously, the databases give the move   71.Bc7  which would render the next two moves totally irrational.  71...Kd5  72.Bb6  Kc4 ?  Mind-boggling, but most likely the game went like this.  73.Bc7 ! =  e3+  74.Ke1 ?  f3  We're back to a simple winning position.  75.Bg3  Kd3  76.Bh4  Bd7  77.Bg3  Bb5  78.Kf1  Kd2+  79.Kg1  Ke2  0-1.   See Ending 41 & Ending 42. }
