{ How should White set up his pieces to defend this position ? } 1. Bc2
{ White must establish the defensive formation against pawns on Black's fourth rank, as the position would become untenable if they were allowed to march onto the fifth.   91.Kg2 ?  A losing move, because we are dealing with a special pawn duo here. The way to go about setting up the pieces against pawns on the fourth rank is Bc2.  e5  92.Bc2  e4 !  93.Ba4  The bishop isn't doing much here, but if it sat on the long diagonal, say on g2, Black would be able to execute the cage.  Kf6  94.Bc6  Ke5  95.Kf2  Bb6+  96.Ke2  f4  97.Kf1  f3  98.Bb7  Kf4  99.Bc6  e3  The pawns are on the sixth rank already. Now the winning manoeuvre, described in 100 Endgames You Must Know, is a piece of cake.  100.Bb5  Ba5  101.Ba6  Ke5  102.Bb5  Kd4  103.Ba6  Kc3  0-1. There would follow ...Kd2 and ...e3-e2. }
1... Kf6 2. Kg2 e5 3. Kf3 { See Ending 41 & Ending 43. } *

