{ Choose between 50... Bxf4 and 50... Re4. } 1... Bxf4
{ inevitably leads to a tough but defensible rook and bishop vs. rook endgame. The alternative loses.   50...Re4 ?  51.Rxe4   51.Kxf5 !  Rxa4  52.Bxa4  also wins, and leads to the same endgame as in the game but without the Black pawn on the e-file. This might look better, but White presumably didn't want to allow the possibility of the rook swinging back. Still, that would also lead to a losing endgame.   51.Ra8 !  keeping the rook, is also winning, e.g.  Ke6  52.Bc6  since  Rxf4 ?  allows  53.Re8#  51...fxe4  52.Kf5  An instructive position. White wins because the Black bishop can't achieve the defensive stand against the pawns, while the Black pawns are to White but a minor nuisance.  e3  53.Be2 !  +-  Controlling both Black pawns.  Bg3  54.Ke4  h5  55.Bxh5  Be1  56.Kxe3  Ba5  57.f5  Be1  58.f6+  Ke6  59.Ke4  Bb4  60.Bg4+  Kf7  61.Kf5  1-0. }
2. Rxf4 Rxe5 { See Ending 43 & Ending 95. } *

