{ Can White successfully deal with all three pawns ? } 1. Ba4
{ White can control all three pawns with a procedure similar to Exercise 122: Blanco-Zavarce. The presence of the third pawn, however, introduces a different element: the bishop now needs to attack the e4-pawn from in front.   66.Bf7 ?  Kd4  -+  The Black king threatens to go to d3, sheltered from any checks by the e-pawn.  67.Ke2  The White king relinquishes control of the crucial c2-square.  Kc3  68.Bd5  b3  69.Bxe4  Kb2 !  70.Bd5  Kc2  0-1.   66.Bg6 ?  Kd4  and the White bishop is cut off by the e4-pawn, making it easy for Black to win by advancing his other pawns. }
1... Kc4 2. Bc2 Kd4
{ 67...Kc3  68.Bxe4  leads to a well-known defensive system. } 3. Ke2
{ and the Black king can't babysit the e4-pawn and, at the same time, advance the b-pawn.  See Ending 47. }
