{ Confusion leads to making the same mistakes. The logical move offered far better resistance.   54.Kd5 !?  fxe5  55.Ke4  f6  56.Bd7  a5  57.Kf3  and we would reach a position in which Black might try one of three different plans. 1. Bringing the king across to support the a-pawn:  Kh6  2. Exchanging the e-pawn for the g-pawn. This plan can only be successful against a careless white player, for example:   57...Kh4  58.Ba4  Bh6  59.Bd7  Bg5  60.Ba4 ?  e4+  61.Kxe4  Kxg4  -+  This would lead to a better endgame compared to the game. However, this plan also fails if White maintains his bishop on the d7- and c6-squares, for example 60. Bc6 !  3. Executing another lengthy manoeuvre with the king:   57...Kh4  58.Ba4  Bh6  59.Bd7  Bg5  60.Bc6 !  Kh3  61.Ba4  Kh2  62.Bd7  Kg1  63.Bb5  (denying access via the f1-square)  Bh4  (zugzwang)  64.Ba4  Kf1  -+  followed by ...Ke1-d2 and the king will support the passed a-pawn.  58.Ke4  Kg7  59.Ba4  Kf8  60.Kd5  Ke7  61.Bb5  Kd8  62.Ba4  Kc7  63.Be8  Kb6  64.Ba4  and it seems that this plan comes to nothing. }
2... fxe5 3. Kxf7 a5
{ The pawn is out of the king's reach, meaning that eventually the bishop will have to let go of the defence of the g4-pawn. }
4. Ke6 a4 5. Kd5 a3 6. Bb1 Kxg4 7. Ke4
{ The Black king is halfway through its journey towards the a-pawn. } 7... Kg3
8. Ba2 Kf2 9. Kd3 Bg5 10. Ke4 Bf6 11. Kd3 Kf3 12. Bd5+ Kf4 13. Ke2 e4 14. Ba2
