{ Choose between 33. g4 and 33. h4. } 1. g4
{ This move leads to a draw, by achieving different pawns to be exchanged. One extra pawn almost never wins in an opposite-coloured bishops endgame. Theoretical interest, therefore, usually departs from a two-pawn surplus. With more pawns on the board, the key to winning such positions is to find the right way to simplify. For instance, by exchanging any and all of the opponent's pawns, to end up with the most promising two vs. zero majority.   33.h4 ?  Not the best move, natural as it may look; it allows Black to fix the pawns and choose which breaks best suit him.  h5 !  This loses by force, as Black will exchange the g-pawn for the h-pawn, and the h-pawn for the g-pawn, creating a special pair of pawns.  34.Ba4  Kf7  35.Kf3  Bb6  36.Bb5  Ba5  37.Bc6  Be1  38.Kf4  e5+  39.Kf3  g5  40.hxg5  Kg6  41.Kg2  Kxg5  42.Kh3  h4  43.gxh4+  Bxh4  44.Kg2  e4  45.Kf1  Kf6  46.Ke2  Ke5  47.Be8  f4  48.Bh5  Bd8  49.Bg4  Bb6  50.Bh5  0-1. }
1... Kf7 2. h4
{ This move is necessary, too, e.g.   34.gxf5  gxf5 !  if   34...exf5  35.h3  White can simply wait  35.Kf3  Kf6  36.Bb3  e5  37.Bc2  Kg5  38.h4+  (Black wins in similar fashion if the h-pawn stays behind)  Kf6  39.h5  Bb6  40.Bb1  Kg5  41.Bc2  This position deserves a diagram to illustrate Black's plan more clearly: White has adopted the correct defensive set-up against the two pawns on Black's fourth rank, and may rely on his bishop giving its life in exchange for both of them, knowing that Black has the wrong bishop:  e4+  42.Bxe4  fxe4+  43.Kxe4  Kg4 !  Zugzwang; the White king must give way, allowing its foe to first capture the h5-pawn, and, after that, block him out of the corner.  44.Kd3  Kxh5  45.Ke2  Kg4  46.Kf1  Kf3  -+  Clearly, were the White pawn further behind, the manoeuvre would be even easier. }
2... Kf6 3. h5 g5 { 35...gxh5  36.gxf5  with an easy draw. } 4. Kf3
{ followed by Bc2 and White can wait.  See Ending 43 } *

