{ Can White win the game by exchanging rooks ? } 1. Rxd6
{ White forces a winning two vs. zero majority. } 1... Kxd6 2. Be8 Bxh4 3. Bxg6
Kc5 4. Bxh5 b5
{ Black exploits the temporary absence of the bishop to exchange the last remaining pawn. This decision, although correct, will not save the game, but otherwise White would play Be8, maintaining the pawn, and win by advancing his other two pawns, forcing the bishop to sacrifice itself for them. }
5. axb5 Kxb5
{ White has achieved special pawns and the Black king is too far away to reach the only defensive stand that could save the game, against White's pawns on the fourth rank. }
6. f4 Kc4 7. Kh3 Be1 8. Kg4 Kd5 9. Kf5 Kd6 10. e4 Ke7 11. Bd1 Bd2 12. Bb3 Bc1
13. Ke5 Bd2 14. Kf5 Bc1 15. e5 Bd2 16. Ke4 Be1 17. f5 Bh4 18. Kf4 Be1 19. f6+
Kf8 20. Kf5 Bb4 21. Ba4 Ba3 22. e6 Bb4 23. Ke4 Ba3 { 1-0.  See Ending 43. } *

