{ Choose between 52... e4 and 52... Bf4. } 1... Bf4
{ is the right plan. As we will see, Black thus obtains an endgame with pawns further separated from one another. To this end, Black keeps his e-pawn where it is, only to later obtain White's a-pawn in return for it, or else force the exchange of the g-pawn in much more favourable circumstances.   52...e4 ?  Black's passers have become quite intimidating. To win, he needs to exchange one of them for the a-pawn, a crucial decision that must be taken right now. This move leads to a clear draw. Black will now be forced to give up his g-pawn in exchange for the a-pawn, resulting in a well-known theoretical draw.  53.Bh1  Bf4  54.Bg2  e3  55.Bf3  Kc3  56.Bd5  Kc2  57.Bb3+  Kb2  58.Bd5  g2  There is no other plan available. Black can't force a better zugzwang, since   58...Kb1  can be answered by moving the king, e.g.  59.Ke1  59.Bxg2  Kxa2  60.Kd3 ?  White spoils all his defensive efforts with this natural-looking move. Both   60.Kd1 !  and   60.Bd5+  b3  61.Kd1  drew.  60...b3  61.Bd5  Ka3  62.Bc4  b2  63.Kc2  e2  0-1. One of the pawns will promote. }
2. Bh1 Kc3 3. Bd5 Kc2 4. Bb3+ Kb2 5. Bd5 { The crucial moment. } 5... e4 6. Be6
{ 57.Bxe4  Kxa2  leads to an endgame with pawns separated by four files, which is always winning. }
6... Bd6 7. Bd5 Be5 8. Be6 Bd4 { Threatens 60... g2. } 9. Kf1 e3 10. Bd5
{ 61.Bc4  Kc3  62.Bb5  Kd2  and the king supports the move ...e3-e2, which will win the bishop. }
10... g2+ 11. Bxg2 Kxa2
{ We've reached a version of this endgame in which the White king can't get to d1 in time: }
12. Ke2 b3 13. Bd5 Ka3 14. Be4 Kb2 15. Bd5 Kc2 { See Ending 47. } *

