{ Does it make any difference whether Black plays 60... Kd8 or 60... Kb8 ? }
1... Kb8
{ As a matter of principle, Black should always prefer the short side, if allowed  (with a central pawn, Black draws either way)  .   60...Kd8 ?  is a mistake easily made, and often unpunished:  61.Rh8+  Ke7  62.Rc8  +-  eventually leads to the Lucena position, just as with a knight's pawn. }
2. Rh8+ Ka7 3. Rd8
{ Azarov-Jarmula, Warsaw rapid 2013, continued with   62.Rc8  Rd1  I always recommend a simpler defensive system with   62...Rh1 !  immediately threatening lateral checks, and if  63.Rd8  Rc1 ! =  going back to Kling and Horwitz's system.  63.Kc7  Rh1  64.Rd8  Rh7+  65.Rd7  Rh8  66.c6  (White has made some progress)  Rg8  67.Rd6  This popular trick, which has claimed some famous victims, we will see again in later exercises.  Rg7+ ?  The automatic mistake; Black is lost.  68.Kc8 !  Rg8+  69.Rd8  Rg6  70.c7  Kb6  71.Kb8  1-0. }
3... Rc2 { 'K&H'. } 4. Kd6 Kb7
{ Draw. White can't make any progress.  See Ending 56. } *

