{ Choose between 55. Rd7+ and 55. Rxd6. } 1. Rd7+
{ 55.Rxd6 ?  White might have assumed that an intermediate check would only help the Black king become more active. However, this in-between check was necessary so as not to fall prey to an ideal horizontal cutoff:  Rxh4  With careful play, Black can transform the current imperfect horizontal cutoff  (winning because of the rook on the h-file)  into an ideal horizontal cutoff.  56.Ke5  Kg7  57.Rd1  Kh6  58.Rg1  g5  And Black has an ideal horizontal cutoff. From here, the winning procedure is straightforward.  59.Kf5  Rf4+  60.Ke5  Kg6  61.Ra1  Rb4  62.Rf1  g4  63.Rf8  Kg5  64.Rg8+  Kh4  65.Kf5  Kh3  66.Rh8+  Kg2  67.Ra8  g3  68.Ra2+  Kh1  0-1. }
1... Kf6 2. Rxd6+ Kf5 3. Kc4 Rxh4+ 4. Kd3
{ and Black can establish neither an ideal horizontal nor a vertical cutoff: }
4... Re4 5. Rxg6 { See Ending 59. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook + Pawn vs. Rook"]
[Black "Georgi Tringov vs. Heinz Liebert"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/6pk/1P1R4/8/2K4p/8/8/1r6 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Choose between 59. Kc5, supporting the passed pawn, or 59. Rd4 to exchange it for the h-pawn. }
1. Kc5
{ Supporting the passed pawn, is correct:   59.Rd4 ?  The right plan is to support the b-pawn, but careful calculation is required.  Rxb6  60.Rxh4+  Rh6 !  This is the point: White can't exchange rooks.  61.Rg4  Rh3 !  The move that guarantees an ideal horizontal cutoff, and with it, easy victory.   61...Rd6 !  cutting the king off vertically by three files, is much more laborious, but also leads to a winning position.  62.Kd4  Kh6  63.Ke4  g5  Reaching the typical position.  64.Kf5  Rf3+  65.Ke4  Rf1  66.Rg2  Kh5  67.Ke3  Kh4  68.Ke2  Rf7  69.Rg1  g4  70.Rh1+  Kg3  0-1. }
1... g5 2. Rd7+
{ 60.Rd3  is also a draw:  Kg6  61.Kc6  g4  62.Rd6+  Kf5  63.Rd5+  Kf4  64.Rb5  Re1  65.Kd7 ! = }
2... Kg6 3. b7 h3 4. Rd6+ Kf5 5. Rb6 Rc1+ 6. Kd4 h2 7. b8=Q h1=Q
{ This position, which would drive a human being mad, is also a draw, according to Lomonosov tablebases.  See Ending 62. }
*

