{ 61...b5 ?  Simplification, the impatient player's hallmark, leads to a theoretically lost endgame.  62.axb5  Rxb5   62...Rxg4  63.b6  Rg7  64.Ka4  +-  63.Rc4  To continue with Rf4 and Rf5.  Kd2   63...Ke3  64.Ka4  Rg5  65.Rc7  Kd4  66.Ra7  Rxg4  67.Rxa5  Kc3+  68.Ka3  transposes to the game.  64.Ka4  Rg5  65.Rc8  Kd3  66.Ra8  Kc3  67.Rxa5  Rxg4+  68.Ka3  Black can't avoid an ideal horizontal cutoff.  Rg1  69.Rc5+  Kd4  70.b4  Ra1+  71.Kb3  Rb1+  72.Ka4  Ra1+  73.Kb5  Ra8  74.Rh5  Rb8+  75.Ka4  Kc4  76.Rc5+  Kd4  77.Kb3  Once more, we have come to an ideal horizontal cutoff. From here, the winning method is a piece of cake.  Ra8  78.Rh5  Rc8  79.b5  Rc5  80.Rh4+  Kd5  81.Kb4  Rc1  82.Rh6  Rb1+  83.Ka5  Kc5  84.Rc6+  Kd5  85.Ka6  Rb2  86.Rh6  Kc5  87.b6  Ra2+  88.Kb7  Rg2  89.Ka7  Kb5  90.b7  Ra2+  91.Kb8  Rc2  92.Rh1  Rg2  93.Ra1  1-0. }
2. Rxb6 { looks like a close shave for Black, but passive defence holds: } 2...
Rg8 3. Rb5 Ra8 4. Kb2
{ and in this position, the Black king is performing a useful role.  See Ending 62. }
