{ Choose between 66. g5 and 66. Kh4. } 1. g5
{ Exchanging pawns, while not the only path to a draw, is simplest, as it leads to a harmless horizontal cutoff that allows a long-side defence.   66.Kh4  leads by force to a queen vs. rook and pawn endgame with a well-known drawing fortress:  Ke5  67.Kg3  Rf3+  68.Kg2 !  Kf4  69.Rh1  e3  70.Rxh6  e2  71.Rf6+  Ke5  72.Rxf3  e1=Q  73.Rf5+ = }
1... hxg5 2. Kxg5 Rf3 3. Kg4 Kd4 4. Ra1
{ To give checks on the long side.   69.Rd1+ ?!  Unnecessarily complicating matters to a certain degree.  Rd3  70.Re1  Rd2  71.Kf4  Rf2+  72.Kg3  Rf8  73.Rd1+  Ke3  74.Re1+  Kd3  75.Rd1+  Ke2  76.Ra1 !  Finally taking the long side, but luckily for the spectators things are about to get exciting.  Rd8  77.Kf4 ??  White seems bent on losing the game.   77.Kg2 ??  was premature on account of  Rg8+  but almost any rook move is good enough.  for example:   77.Ra2+  Rd2  78.Ra1  e3  79.Kg2 =  The rook in the corner, lord of the long side, ensures the draw.  77...e3  78.Ra2+  Rd2  79.Ra3  Rd4+ ??  Black's blunder is probably due to a lack of faith in his position.   79...Rd3 !  80.Ra2+  Ke1  wins easily.  80.Kg3  Rd3  81.Ra2+ ??  A losing check, seen before in similar positions.   81.Ra1 !  was the correct defence.  81...Ke1  With the king on the back rank and the rook on the third  (preventing Kf3)  the position is winning.  82.Ra1+  Rd1 !  83.Ra2  e2  84.Kg2  Rb1 ?!  Black also seems determined to do things in the most complicated way.   84...Rd8  with the idea of ...Rg8+, wins easily.  85.Kf3  Rb3+  86.Kg2  A fairly typical position that allows only one winning move.  Rc3 ??  Such a typical error. In my database there are four games with the same position  (symmetrical positions and positions with colours reversed included)  before this move, and in all of them the winning side erred ! The simple winning move is   86...Re3 !  -+  87.Ra1+  Kd2  88.Kf2 !  White is back on the bus to Share-point City.  Rc2  89.Re1  Kd3  90.Ra1  Rc3  91.Ke1  Ke3  92.Ra3 !  Fastest.  Rd3  93.Rb3  Ke4  94.Rb2  Winning the pawn.  Kf3  95.Rxe2  Re3  96.Rxe3+  Kxe3  Draw. }
4... Rd3 5. Kf4
{ now preventing ...Ke3 or ...e4-e3  is the easiest defensive technique.  See Ending 59, Ending 64 & Ending 99. }
*

