{ Choose between 67. Rb1, 67. Ke4 and 67. Ke5. } 1. Rb1
{ Both a horizontal and a vertical cutoff are possible  (or so it seems)  . Only a player well-versed in the characteristics of each kind of cutoff is able to find the right moves. Watch how the legendary endgame specialist Ulf Andersson handles the position. Ulf was famous for lulling his victims into a false sense of security, beyond the point of no return. After this move, Black gets an ideal horizontal cutoff.   67.Ke4 !  not only avoids a horizontal cutoff, it also renders a vertical one toothless, since with the knight's pawn, two files are insufficient to carry out Grigoriev's winning method:  b5  68.Rb1  Kb6  and White's pieces are placed ideally, but only one move holds:  69.Ke3 ! =   67.Ke5 ?  is on its face a good move, as it brings the king closer. There is, however, an important tactical nuance, allowing Black to create a horizontal cutoff:  Rc6 !  There it is, White can't enter a pawn endgame. Perhaps White thought that the cutoff would be imperfect and that the rook might be able to swing across to the other side, but it's a mirage:  68.Rb1  or   68.Ra1  Rc4  69.Kd5  b5 !  68...Rc4  69.Kd5  Ra4  and Black reaches a theoretically winning position in either variation. }
1... Rd4 { Of course, Ulf seizes his chance. } 2. Ke5 Ra4 3. Kd5 Ka6 4. Kc6
Rc4+ 5. Kd5 b5 { Once again we've reached an ideal horizontal cutoff. } 6. Ra1+
Kb6 7. Ra2 Rh4 8. Ra8 b4 9. Rb8+ Ka5 10. Kc5 Ka4 11. Rg8 Ka3 12. Ra8+ Kb2 13.
Ra4 Kc3 { 0-1.  See Ending 59, Ending 60, Ending 61 & Ending 62. } *

