{ Is the extra pawn enough to win the game ? } 1... Re4
{ A vertical cutoff ensures victory. All necessary circumstances are met: 1. The king can be cut off by two files. 2. The pawn can get to Black's fourth rank before the White rook achieves the right defensive set-up on the back rank. And 3. We're dealing with a bishop's pawn. }
2. Kf3 { 61.Rh1  seems more normal, but eventually leads to the same. } 2...
Re1
{ It's useful to provoke the White king into moving down a rank. Other moves, such as   61...Re6  or   61...Re7  also win. }
3. Kf2 Re6 4. Rh1 c5
{ We've reached an important position: White's king separated by two files allows Black to comfortably execute Grigoriev's combined method. }
5. Rb1+ Kc6 6. Rc1 Kb5 7. Rb1+ Ka4 8. Rc1 Kb4 9. Rb1+ Ka3 10. Rc1 Re5
{ Completing the manoeuvre. White's king can't harass the rook, but if it were any closer, ...Rc6 would also win. }
11. Kf3 Kb2 { 0-1.  See Ending 60. } *

