{ Choose between 74... Kf5 and 74... Kf3. } 1... Kf3
{ had to be played:   74...Kf5 ?  The two rare motifs featured in this position confused even an experienced grandmaster. Black's pieces head for rear-guard positions, but will fail to occupy the necessary defensive posts.  75.Rf1+  Ke6  76.Kxg4  Rxh2  77.Kg5 !  An unusual but tried-and-tested plan. White's king moves up the board and keeps his rook ready to interpose once the enemy rook starts checking from the front.  Rh8  78.Rf6+ !  Ke7  79.g4  Rg8+  80.Rg6  The rook intervenes.  Ra8  Black now lacks one tempo to play   80...Kf7  and draw the game. A database search for similar positions reveals all sorts of results: unobjectionable wins, missed draws, and squandered winning chances. In this game, White executed the next tricky phase with impeccable technique.  81.Rg7+  Kf8  82.Kh6  Ra6+  83.Kh7  Ra4  84.g5  Rh4+  85.Kg6  Rg4 ?!   85...Ra4  is the most logical move but lacks one tempo to reach the bank-rank defence:  86.Rb7 !   86.Rf7+ ??  Kg8  87.Rf6  Ra8 =  86...Ra6+  87.Kh7  +-  86.Rf7+  Kg8  87.Ra7  Kf8  88.Ra8+  Ke7  89.Rg8  1-0. }
2. Rf1+ Kg2 3. Rf4 Kxh2 4. Kxg4 Ra8 5. Rf2+ Kg1
{ The rook performs an unusual but effective version of long-distance checking, for example: }
6. Rc2 Rg8+ 7. Kf3 Rf8+ 8. Ke3 Re8+
{ and White's king can run, but it can't hide.  See Ending 59. } *

