{ Black can choose between taking the a6-pawn or the b3-pawn, after 56... Rb6+. Which move is correct ? }
1... Rxa6
{ is the right move, leading to either a Philidor position or an insufficient vertical cutoff:   56...Rb6+ ?  Even though capturing the b3-pawn is natural  (to leave the opponent with a rook's pawn)  , the tactics favour White. Good decision-making involves considering different types of theoretical endgames in combination with careful calculation. Here, eliminating the far-advanced a-pawn is more important than capturing the b3-pawn.  57.Ka5  Rxb3  58.Rxg2   58.a7 !  Ra3+  59.Kb6  Rb3+  60.Kc5  Ra3  61.Rxg2  leads to the same thing.  58...Ra3+  59.Kb6  Rb3+  60.Kc5   60.Kc6  Rc3+  61.Kb5  also wins, because of the same motif as in the main variation: the Black rook isn't far enough to keep giving checks. In this position, Black would be able to make a draw if his rook were further away  (e. g. on b1)  by giving checks incessantly. Since this is not possible here, all Black can do is move the rook behind the pawn, allowing White to exploit a tactical resource.  60...Ra3 !  61.a7 !  Ke6  Black's king tries to threaten the pawn by getting off the seventh rank, but it's too little too late.   61...Kf6  62.Kb6  Rb3+  63.Ka6  Ra3+  64.Kb7  Rb3+  65.Ka8  Ke7  66.Rg8  and we reach a familiar winning position:   61...Kd7  62.Rg8 !  62.Rg7  Ra1  63.Kc6  Ra2  64.Kb7  Rb2+  65.Kc8  Ra2  66.Kb8  Rb2+  67.Rb7  Rh2  68.a8=Q  Rh8+  69.Ka7  1-0. }
2. Rxg2 Kd7 3. Rc2
{ 58.Kb5  Re6  59.Rc2  Re8  60.Rc6  Rb8+  61.Rb6  Rh8 =  Intending ...Kc7. }
3... Rb6+ 4. Ka4 Rb8
{ A vertical cutoff by one file is not enough.  See Ending 67. } *

