{ Is 68. Rd8 enough to make a draw ? } 1. Rd8
{ Absolute precision is required in this position: after 68. Rd8, the rook won't be in time to go to the long side.   68.Re8  achieving Kling & Horwitz's defence, is the easiest way to draw the game. }
1... Rf2+
{ This check is essential to get the rook to the other side with tempo. In the game Asilkefelli-Erdogan, Kemer 2012, Black let victory slip through his hands after   68...Rh1+ ?  (move 63 in that game)  69.Kg2  Re1  70.Ra8 ! = }
2. Ke1 Ra2
{ The only move, denying the enemy rook access to the long side, by going there himself. Many games have followed less successful paths, such as Paulovic-Stork, Ricany 2011:   69...Rf4 ?  (move 72 in that game)  70.Rd7  Kf3  71.Rd4 ?  the losing move;   71.Ra7  and many other moves, would hold the draw  71...Rg4 !  -+  72.Kf1  Rh4  73.Kg1  Rh5  74.Rd8  e3  75.Rf8+  Ke2  76.Rd8  Rg5+  77.Kh2  Ke1  78.Ra8  e2  79.Rf8  Rd5  80.Rg8  Kd1  0-1. }
3. Kf1 Ra1+ 4. Kg2 Ke2
{ Because of the bad position of White's rook, the Black pawn will reach its sixth rank. }
5. Rd7
{ The perhaps more natural variation   72.Rb8  e3  73.Rb2+  Kd3  74.Rb3+  Kd2  75.Rb2+  Kc3 !  shows that White's rook lacks effective checking distance. }
5... e3 6. Rd8 Ra7
{ Now the Black rook prepares to kick the enemy king away by means of a check on the g-file. }
7. Rd6 { If   74.Rg8  Ke1  wins easily. } 7... Rg7+ 8. Kh3 Kf2 9. Rf6+ Ke1 10.
Re6 e2 11. Rd6 Rg5 12. Kh4 Rg1 { 0-1.  See Ending 56, Ending 57 & Ending 58. }
