{ Choose between 46... a5 and 46... Rxh2. } 1... a5
{ While not the only way to draw, this is probably best. The idea is to attack the b3-pawn, without losing the a6-pawn with check:   46...Rxh2 ?  The fight is about cutting off the Black king as far away as possible; two files isn't enough, but three is.  47.Rxa6+ !  Winning.  Kf7  48.Kd5 !  It's too late for Black to take any satisfactory defensive stand.  Ke7   48...Rh8  49.b4  Rd8+  50.Rd6  Rb8  51.Kc5  and the pawn will cross the middle of the board, and this, as we know, inevitably leads to Lucena.  49.Kc6 !  Rc2+  50.Kb7  Kd7  51.Rh6  Rb2  52.Rb6  White is ready to move his pawn forward.  Rc2  53.b4  Rc7+  54.Kb8  Rc1  55.b5  Rc8+  56.Ka7  Rc1  57.Rb7+  Kc8  58.Rb8+  Kd7  59.b6  Ra1+  60.Kb7  Rb1  61.Rh8  Ke7  62.Rh2  Kd6  63.Rc2  1-0. The Lucena position is imminent, therefore Black resigned. }
2. Kd4 Rb2 3. Kc3 { 48.Kc4  Rc2+ ! } 3... Rxh2
{ and now the endgame is a draw, for example: } 4. Rxa5 Kf6 5. Rd5 Ke6 6. Rd1
Rh8 { With a knight's pawn, a two-file cutoff is insufficient. } *

