{ Does liquidating the pawns lead to victory ? } 1... Kd4
{ Simplification is a mistake, as the White rook is on the long side. All moves that keep the c6-pawn win:   59...Kd6 !  60.Rd2+  Ke6  61.Rc2  Rg1+  62.Kh5  Kd6  63.Rc4  Rf1  64.Kg5  Rf4  -+   59...Kb6  -+  60.Rb2+  Kc7  61.Rc2  Kd6  62.Rd2+  Ke6 !  63.Rc2  Rg1+  64.Kh5  Kd6  -+ }
2. Rxc6 Kxe4 3. Rc4+ Kd3
{ The White king is cut off and the pawn is ready to advance, but: } 4. Ra4
{ Showing good defensive technique by going to the long side. } 4... e4 5. Ra3+
{ Time to start giving checks.   63.Kg4 ?  would lose on account of  e3  64.Ra3+  Kd2 !  65.Ra2+  Ke1  66.Ra1+  Kf2  67.Ra2+  e2  -+ }
5... Ke2
{ The king tries crossing the e-file. Should it stay on the queenside, then White would just keep checking. Now is the time to bring the White king closer. }
6. Kg4 e3 { Now White ought to continue with } 7. Kg3
{ but instead he played:   65.Ra2+ ?  Ke1  66.Kg3  e2  67.Kg2    Reaching Exercise 142. }
7... Rg1+ 8. Kf4 Rf1+ 9. Kg3 Rd1 10. Kg2 Rd2 11. Ra1
{ See Ending 54 & Ending 57. } *

