{ Choose between 63... Ke3 and 63... Ke4. } 1... Ke4
{ 63...Ke3 ?  The king is cut off, and the pawn has crossed the middle of the board. Does it really matter whether Black plays 63... Ke3 or 63... Ke4 ? Yes, it does ! White has a defence against one of these moves, based on an interesting resource well worth remembering.  64.Ra5  Kf3  The key variation is   64...Rf3  65.Ra4 !  g3  66.Rg4  reaching an unusual position in which Black can't rearrange his pieces without allowing Philidor's Defence:  Kd3  67.Rg8  Ke4  68.Rg7  Kf4  69.Rg8  (giving checks also draws)  and it becomes clear that if Black continues  Ra3  there follows  70.Kf1  ending in Philidor's Defence.  65.Kf1  Kg3+  66.Kg1  Draw. White achieves the back-rank defence. }
2. Ra5 Rf3 3. Ra4+ Kf5 4. Ke2 Kg5
{ would lead to the Lucena position.  See Ending 55. } *

