{ Choose between 51. Ke3 and 51. Re4. } 1. Re4
{ prevents the king from making progress, and if Black intends to capture the pawn, White has several defences, for example:   51.Ke3 ?  This leads to defeat. White can't keep the b-pawn, nor otherwise execute any known defensive method of play. Many rook vs. rook and pawn positions are theoretically lost, and over-confidence with some players causes them to lose even more often. In this position, the key for White is to assess the right moment to part with the b2-pawn.  Kb4  52.Rd2  Rc5  53.Kd4  Rc8  54.Rh2  Kb3  55.Kd3  Rd8+  56.Ke3  b4  The position is of theoretical interest: the b2-pawn is about to drop and there is no way to get any known defensive construct in return for it.  57.Rh4  Rd1  58.Ke2  Rc1  59.Kd2  Rc2+  60.Kd1  Rxb2  Reaching an almost identical position we have seen several times before: the pawn is unstoppable.  61.Kc1  Ka2  62.Rh8  Rb1+  63.Kc2  b3+  64.Kc3  Rc1+  65.Kb4  Rg1  66.Kc3  Rg3+  67.Kb4  b2  68.Ra8+  Kb1  69.Rc8  Rg7  70.Kc3  Ra7  0-1. }
1... Rd2 2. Ke3 Rxb2 3. Kd3 { and the White king is in time.  See Ending 53. }
