{ Can White hold his own ? } 1. Kf1
{ White can hold by means of a simple variation that, however, requires a keen understanding of the back-rank defence. }
1... Ra2 { A difficult choice. } 2. Rg6
{ would have demonstrated a deep understanding of the position. White's rook prevents the pawn from moving forward while his king correctly takes up residence on g1. After that, the rook will transfer to the back rank, e.g.   56.Rb6 ?  Kg3 !  Now White's king has no time for Kg1.  57.Rb3+  An echo of Philidor's Defence, which here doesn't work.  Kh2 !  58.Rb4  Rg2  59.Rc4  g4  60.Rc8  Rg1+  61.Ke2  g3  0-1. By now a familiar sight: the Lucena position is about to appear on the board. }
