{ White's king is in check. Does it make any difference where it moves to ? }
1. Kd1
{ is easiest. Had White played this move, then he might have avoided his later error:   45.Ke1 ??  is losing. In this variation we see how one's own pawn can sabotage an otherwise valid defensive system. White's rook can't carry out Philidor's Defence as it is unable to give checks from behind:  e3  46.f5  Kd3 !  (gaining a crucial tempo)  47.Ra3+  Ke4  48.f6  Kf3  (and now another one)  49.Kd1  Rd2+  50.Ke1  Rb2  51.Kd1  Kf2  -+   45.Kf1 !?  e3   45...Ke3  46.Ra3+  Kxf4 =  Philidor.  46.f5  White should get rid of this pawn to reach a pure Philidor position, and he seems to be well on his way to doing so.  Kd3  47.Re5 ??   47.Ra3+  Ke4  48.f6  (White needs only one more tempo, for either f6-f7 or Ra5, to start the second stage of Philidor's Defence)  Kf3  (the most tenacious)  49.Kg1 !  Rg2+  50.Kf1 !  Rb2  51.Ra1 !  Rb7  52.f7  Rxf7  53.Ra8 =  preparing checks from behind.  47...e2+  48.Kg1  Rh4 !  The only winning move.   48...Rh5 ?  49.Kf2  forces a repetition of moves:  Rh2+  and White has an additional option:  50.Kf3 ! =  49.Kg2  Rf4 !  50.Rd5+  Ke4  51.Rd8  Rxf5  52.Re8+  Kd3  53.Rd8+  Ke3  54.Re8+  Kd2  55.Rd8+  Ke1  Reaching Lucena.  56.Rd7  Rg5+  57.Kh2  Kf2  58.Rf7+  Ke3  59.Re7+  Kf3  0-1. }
1... e3 2. f5 Kd3 3. Ra3+ Ke4 4. f6
{ Black is forced to deal with the far-advanced pawn, after which we are in the final stage of Philidor's Defence.  See Ending 52 & Ending 53. }
