{ Choose between 78. Kd7+, 78. Ke7+ and 78. Rd5. } 1. Ke7+
{ Faced with the imminent loss of half his pawn majority, White must seize the moment to transform the position into a theoretically winning endgame. Knowing some theory about a pawn on the sixth rank comes in handy: the game is won if the stronger side's king reaches the eighth rank without offering an exchange of rooks.   78.Kd7+ ??  Not good enough to win, as it doesn't prevent Black from setting up a long-side defence.  Kxg5  79.e5  Ra1 ??  Surprisingly, trying to set up the long-side defence is premature. First, the king has to be improved:   79...Re1 !  80.e6  Kf6  81.Rd2  Ra1 !  . Now is the time to bring the rook to the long side:  82.Rf2+  Kg7  83.e7  Ra7+ !  80.e6  Ra7+  81.Ke8  Kf6  82.e7+  Kg7  83.Rd1  Kf6  84.Kf8  1-0.   78.Rd5 ?  keeps both pawns, but the White rook is now very passive, allowing Black a long-side defence:  Ra1 ! = }
1... Kxg5 { The Black king is just a tad offside. } 2. e5 Ra1 3. e6
{ Now Black would need his rook on the eighth rank and his king on g6, but he lacks the time to achieve this: }
3... Kg6 4. Ke8 { The only winning move. } 4... Ra8+ 5. Rd8 Ra6 6. e7 Kf6 7.
Kf8 { See Ending 54 & Ending 57. } *

