{ Choose between 66... Rf8+ and 66... Kd6. } 1... Rf8+
{ The defending side must immediately harass the rook on the seventh rank lest it build a bridge  (see the variation at move 67)  .   66...Kd6 !  is necessary, and if  67.Re4  Kd5 ! }
2. Kg3 Kd6
{ 67...Rg8  seems a logical alternative, but is met by  68.Kf4  Rf8+  69.Kg5  Rg8+  70.Kf5  Rf8+  71.Kg6  Rg8+  72.Rg7  building a bridge. }
3. Re4 Kd5 4. Rf4 Rg8 5. Rf6
{ White has transformed an inefficient vertical cutoff by two files into an imperfect horizontal cutoff, and is now winning the game since Black's king can't reach the h-file. It's a pity that White fails to correctly follow up on his excellent play. }
5... Ke5 6. Rh6
{ would force an ideal horizontal cutoff within a few moves.   71.Ra6 ?  Allowing the opponent to go to an adjacent file of the pawn.  Rh8 !  Black grabs his last chance.  72.Rb6  Rh1  73.Rc6  Rh8  74.g5  Kf5  75.Rc5+  Kg6  76.Kg4  Rh1  77.Rc6+  Kg7  78.Rc7+  Draw.   See Ending 62. }
