{ Can White defend this position ? } 1. Ra2
{ Against doubled knight's pawns, the defender can save the game by placing the rook on the second rank. In this position particularly, it is the only way to draw. Let's see why other moves lose.   51.Ra1 ?  The back-rank defence doesn't work against two pawns.  Rb2+ !  52.Kg1  Kh3 !  53.Rd1  All White can do is wait.  g3  54.Ra1  g5  55.Rc1  g4  56.Ra1  Rb3  57.Rf1  g2  58.Rc1  Rf3  Followed by 59... Rf1, winning the pawn endgame.   51.Ra8 ?  Rb2+  52.Kg1  Kh3  53.Rh8+  Kg3  -+ }
1... g3+ 2. Kg2 Rb4 3. Ra8 Rb2+ 4. Kg1 Kg4 5. Rg8 g5 6. Rf8 g2
{ The only practical chance worth a try. } 7. Rc8
{ Draw.   57.Rh8 ?  A bad square. The White rook must be ready not only to give checks from behind, but also to return to the back rank.  Rb1+ !  Now the position is winning, as seen several times in the previous chapter. The rest of the game is totally incomprehensible and best left uncommented.  58.Kxg2  Rb2+  59.Kf1  Kg3  60.Ra8  Rb1+  61.Ke2  g4  62.Rg8  Ra1  63.Rg7  Ra8  64.Kf1  Rb8  65.Rg6  Rf8+  66.Kg1  Ra8  67.Kf1  Ra1+  68.Ke2  Kh3  69.Kf2  Ra2+  70.Kg1  Kg3  71.Kf1  Rh2  72.Kg1  Rc2  -+   See Ending 70. }
*

