{ Choose between 62... Ra7 and 62... Ra1. } 1... Ra1
{ Places the rook in the opposite corner, ready to give checks either from behind or from the side, as the situation may require.   62...Ra7 ?  If White's king were allowed to go to g5, Black wouldn't be in time to give any checks. White, meanwhile, would still be making progress, as the game continuation shows.  63.Kg5  Rb7  64.Re5 !  White prepares f5-f6. The direct   64.f6 ?  fails because of  Rb5+ !  64...Rb1  It's too late for this, but the game was lost anyway.   64...Ra7  65.f6 !  +-  65.Re7+  Kg8  66.f6  We've reached a position with the king cut off on the back rank.  Rg1+  67.Kf5  Rf1+  68.Ke6  Re1+  69.Kd7  Rf1  The position now requires accurate play as well as theoretical knowledge of some Chapter 9 endgames.  70.Re8+  Kh7  71.Re6  Kxh6  72.Ke8 !  The only winning move.   72.Ke7 ?  Kg6 ! =  72...Rf2  73.f7+  Kg7  74.Re1  1-0.  This is the only correct defensive plan, and this move, together with   62...Ra2  and   62...Ra3  are the only drawing moves available to Black. }
2. Kg5 Rg1+ { See Ending 58, Ending 71 & Ending 73. } *

