[SetUp "1"]

{ Choose between 84. Ra8 and 84. Kg2. } 1. Kg2
{ is the only move to draw.   84.Ra8 ?  It's natural to bring the rook to the opposite corner, ready to fire away checks at the king. In this position, however, the timing isn't right for this move. The fact that the kings are in opposition means that Black can now force the White monarch onto the back rank.  Rb2+  Once the defending king is on the back rank, the position is almost always winning. The procedure takes some time, but is relatively easy.  85.Kg1  Rd2  86.Ra3  Kg4  87.Rb3  f4  The best plan is to get both pawns to their sixth rank.  88.Ra3  h4  89.Rb3  h3  90.Rb8  f3  Once the pawns are on the sixth, White's king, bombarded by checks, uses its own rook to find shelter. Then, all you need is just a little patience.  91.Rg8+  Kf4  92.Rf8+  Ke3  93.Re8+  Kd3  94.Rd8+  Ke2  95.Re8+  Kd1  96.Rf8  Ke2  97.Re8+  Kd1  98.Rf8  Rg2+  99.Kh1  Rf2  100.Kg1  Ke1  101.Ra8  Rd2  0-1. }
1... Rb2+ 2. Kh3 Kf3 3. Rg3+ Ke4 4. Rg8 f4 5. Rh8 Kf3 6. Rxh5 Kf2 7. Ra5 f3 8.
Ra1
{ reaching a corner of the board with enough checking distance.  See Ending 58, Ending 71 & Ending 73. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook + Two Pawns vs. Rook"]
[Black "Alexander Grischuk vs. Pavel Eljanov"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/2k5/8/PKP5/1R6/8/8/7r b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Only one move saves Black. Which one ? } 1... Kb7
{ White has a dominant position. Black had better know his stuff, or he will soon be toast. In the diagram position, Black's king must be ready to block either pawn coming to the sixth rank. Then, the Black rook should be in a position to fire away checks in any direction required. Eljanov found the first good move, but erred later on.  Examples of bad ones come by the dozen:   63...Rg1 ?  Interestingly, in this case, the player with the Black pieces saved the game:  64.a6 !?  (moving the a-pawn forward is one of several dangerous plans)  Ra1  65.Ra4  Rb1+  66.Kc4  Rc1+  67.Kb5   67.Kb4 !  Rb1+  68.Kc3  is the winning continuation  67...Rb1+  68.Ka5 ? =  Kc6  69.Ra3  Rb2  70.Ra1  Rb3  71.Ka4  Rb2  72.Ka3  Rb5  73.Rh1  Ra5+  74.Kb4  Rxa6  Draw, Charbonneau-Moskvitch, Montreal 2000. }
2. a6+ Ka7
{ In accordance with the rule saying that the king must be placed in front of the pawn that advances to the sixth rank. }
3. Rd4 Rb1+ 4. Kc6 Rh1 5. Rd8 { Reaching another critical moment. } 5... Rh2
{ Would still draw.   67...Rh5 ?  The kind of error made by a player who has got tired after defending for a long time. That said, knowing one's endgame theory would of course help. Black should have kept his rook in the corner to be able to choose between different checking options.  68.Kb5 !  There are no checks from behind. White is now winning, and Grischuk doesn't let his opponent off the hook.  Rh7  69.Rd5  Preparing c5-c6 followed by Rd7.  Rh1  70.Rd7+  Kb8  71.c6  We've reached a position with the king cut off on the back rank and pawns on the sixth, which we know is winning.  Rb1+  72.Kc5  Rc1+  73.Kb6  Rb1+  74.Kc5  Rc1+  75.Kd6  Rd1+  76.Ke7  Rc1  77.Rd8+  Ka7  78.Kd7  Rd1+  79.Kc8  1-0.   67...Rg1 !  Also draws.   67...Rh3 !  Also draws.   See Ending 71 & Ending 73. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook + Two Pawns vs. Rook"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen vs. Vladimir Kramnik"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "6k1/8/7P/R7/6r1/6P1/6K1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Choose between 48... Rg6 and 48... Kh7. } 1... Rg6
{ Black can force a drawn position against two connected pawns. Kramnik's display of defensive skill in this blitz game is impressive.   48...Kh7 ?  seems more natural, but after  49.Ra6  there is no way to prevent the white pawn from coming to g5, e.g.  Rg8  50.Kh3  Rg5  51.Kh4  Rg8  52.g4  Rb8  53.Kh5  and White wins easily. This variation shows that Black needs the king on g6 to make a draw against this particular pawn duo. }
2. Rh5 Kh7 3. Kh3 Rf6 4. Kh4 Rg6 5. g4 Ra6 6. g5 Ra4+ 7. Kg3 Ra8
{ Not the only move to draw; Black immediately sets about improving the position of his king. }
8. Rh4 Kg6 { The king stands well here. } 9. Rg4 Ra3+ 10. Kh4 Ra6 11. Re4 Rb6
12. Kg4 Ra6 13. Kf4
{ The position is nearly identical to diagram 11.10 in 100 Endgames You Must Know. }
13... Rb6 14. Re7 Rb4+ 15. Ke5 Rb5+ 16. Ke4 { Draw.  See Ending 74. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook + Two Pawns vs. Rook"]
[Black "Manuel Rivas Pastor vs. Jesus de la Villa Garcia"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/1k6/8/8/1P5R/7P/1r6/6K1 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Can Black somehow save the game ? } 1... Rb3
{ Vancura's Defence is often possible against two pawns, too - provided one of the pawns is a rook's pawn. This is what Black should have used to save the game here.  The game continued   54...Kc6 ?  55.Rd4 !  Kb5  56.h4  and by laterally defending both pawns, White wins easily. This was a rapid game and no other score sheet has survived but a vague remembrance in my mind. }
2. Kf2 Kb6
{ The Black king should head for the b4-pawn, for example:   55...Kb8 ?  56.Ke2  Kb7  57.Kd2  Kb6  58.Kc2  Rg3  59.Rh5  +-  Rf3  60.h4  Rf4  61.Kb3  Rg4  62.Ka4  +- }
3. Ke2 Kb5 4. Kd2 Rg3 5. Kc2 Rf3 { See Ending 76. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook + Two Pawns vs. Rook"]
[Black "Krister Lagerborg vs. Stefan Schneider"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "R3K3/5rk1/8/P7/7P/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Choose between 67. Rc8, 67. Rd8 and 67. Ra6. } 1. Rc8
{ This is the correct move.   67.Ra6 ?  White must avoid Vancura's Defence against two pawns. With this in mind, he should immediately get his rook off the a-file, where it blocks his a-pawn. However, not just any move will do.  Rf5 !  Black's rook immediately attacks the a-pawn from the side, all in accordance with Vancura. From now on, White's rook is uncomfortably stuck in front of its own pawn.  68.Ke7  Rf7+  69.Kd8  Rf8+  70.Kc7  Rf7+  71.Kb6  Rf6+  72.Kb5  Rf5+  73.Kb4  Rf4+  74.Kb3  Rf3+   74...Rxh4 ?  would be too greedy, giving White's rook time to improve its position:  75.Re6  Kf7  76.Re1  Rg4  77.a6  +-  75.Kc2  Rf2+  76.Kd3  Now Black has only one move: the rook must attack the pawn laterally again.  Rf5 ! =  77.Ra7+  Kg6  78.a6  Rf6  79.Ra8  Kg7  80.Ke4  Kh7  81.Ke5  Rb6  Draw. Black has demonstrated excellent defensive skill; White recognized this after sixteen more moves.  By contrast,   67.Rd8 ?  wouldn't work:  Rf8+  68.Ke7  Rf5  69.a6  Rf7+  70.Ke8  Rf8+  71.Kd7  Rf6 !  White's rook is forced to defend the pawn from a8, allowing Black to get back into the Vancura position:  72.Ra8  Kh7 = }
1... Rf8+ { 67...Ra7  68.Rc5   67...Rf5  68.a6  Ra5  69.Rc7+  Kf6  70.a7  +- }
2. Kd7 Rf5 3. a6 { See Ending 76. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Rook + Two Pawns vs. Rook"]
[Black "Alexander Kotov vs. Salo Flohr"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "R7/8/8/5pk1/6r1/7p/5K2/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Choose between 80. Rb8 and 80. Rg8. } 1. Rb8
{ In another game, Smyslov, already at the ripe old age of sixty-eight, demonstrated crisp knowledge of this difficult endgame and played this waiting move.   80.Rg8+ ?  White must wait with the rook in the corner. Giving a hasty check is tempting but wrong: even a great player such as Kotov can't resist the temptation.  Kf4  81.Rh8  Rg2+  82.Kf1  Kg3  83.Rg8+  Kf3   83...Kh2 !  was the way to win.  84.Rh8  Let's look at another defensive plan:   84.Ra8  Rb2  85.Ra3+  Kg4  86.Kg1  f4   and Black has basically achieved  Position 11.5 from 100 Endgames You Must Know:  87.Rc3  f3  88.Rc8  Rb1+  89.Kh2  f2  90.Rf8  Rb2  91.Kh1  Kg5  92.Rg8+  Kf4  93.Rf8+  Ke3  94.Re8+  Kd2  0-1 Valden-E.Atalik, Chalkis 2010.  84...Kg3  85.Rg8+  Kf3 ?  Black can't find the right procedure. Undeniably, unless one knows the position well, voluntarily squeezing oneself into that awkward little mouse hole in front of one's own pawn is not an easy decision to make.  86.Rh8  Rg3  87.Rh7  Ke3  88.Rh8  Kf3  89.Rh4  f4  90.Rh8  Kg4  91.Rg8+  Kf5  92.Rf8+  Ke4  93.Re8+  Kf3  94.Rh8  Ke3  95.Rh7  Kd2  96.Kf2  Re3  97.Rd7+ ?!   97.Ra7 !  Bringing the rook to the long side, is correct.  97...Rd3  Now White has one move to draw the game, and not a particularly natural one:  98.Rh7 ?  Difficult to resist the temptation, but getting behind the pawn is a mistake.   98.Re7 !  Kd1  99.Re1+  Kc2  100.Re7  h2  101.Kg2  Re3  102.Ra7 =  98...Kd1  99.Kf1  Rf3+  100.Kg1  Ke2  101.Ra7  Rd3  102.Re7+  Re3  103.Ra7  Rd3  104.Re7+  Kf3  105.Kh2  Kf2  106.Ra7  The rook is on the long side, but the h3-pawn is still on the board.  Re3  107.Ra1  Re1  108.Ra2+  Kf1 !  Without the h-pawn, this position would be a draw after 109. Ra3, stopping the f-pawn in its tracks. With the h-pawn on the board, however, this doesn't work.  109.Kxh3   109.Ra3  Re2+ !  110.Kh1  Re3  111.Ra1+  Re1  112.Ra3  f3 !  This very unpleasant tactic is another reason for White's demise.  109...Re3+ !  110.Kg4  f3  111.Kg3  f2+  112.Kh2  Re8  113.Ra1+  Ke2  114.Ra2+  Kf3  0-1. }
1... Rh4 { 80...h2  81.Rg8+ ! = } 2. Kg1 Re4 3. Kh2 Re3 4. Rg8+ Kf4 5. Ra8 Ke4
6. Ra4+ Ke5 7. Ra5+ Kf6 8. Ra8 f4 9. Ra5 Re5 10. Ra6+ Kf5 11. Kxh3 Re2 12. Ra4
Rd2 { Draw, Smyslov-Large, London 1989.  See Ending 73. } *

