{ Choose between 54...Rh3+ and 54...Rh4. } 1... Rh4
{ This is the move based on Vancura's Defence, attacking the pawn that is being protected by the rook.   54...Rh3+ ?  How strange to see a player lose like this in a correspondence game, in such a simple position.  55.Kc4  Rh4+  56.Kc5 ?  White returns the favour.  By defending the pawn with   56.Kb5 !  White would have a unique opportunity to free his rook:  Rxh5+   56...Kxh5  57.Ra8  +-  is easier  57.Kb6  Rh4  58.Ra6  Kg7  59.a5  +-  56...Rf4 !  Suddenly, Black wakes up and carries out the defensive method he disregarded on move 54. By keeping the pawn under attack, while preparing to give checks from the side, White won't be able to improve his rook.   56...Re4 ?  is not good enough:  57.Ra8  Rf4   57...Kg7  58.a5  Re5+  59.Kb6  Re6+  60.Kc7  Re5  61.a6  +-  58.a5  Rf5+  59.Kb6  Rf6+  60.Kc7  Rf5  61.a6  Rf6  62.a7  +-  57.Kd6   57.Ra8  Kh7 !  58.a5  Rf5+  59.Kb6  Rf6+  60.Kc7  Rf5  61.a6  Rf6 =  57...Rd4+  58.Ke5  Rc4  59.Ra8  Rb4  60.a5  Rb5+  61.Kd6  Kg7  62.Ra7+  Kh6  63.Kc6  Rf5  64.Ra6  Kg7  65.Ra7+  Kh6  66.a6  Rf6+  67.Kd5  Rb6  The rook keeps the a-pawn under attack. Once the king comes to its rescue, the latter becomes the new target.  68.Ra8  Kg7  69.Ra7+  Kh6  70.Kc5  Re6  71.Kb5  Re5+  72.Kc6  Re6+  73.Kd7  Rf6  74.Ra8  Kg7  75.a7  Rf7+  76.Ke6  Rf6+  77.Ke5  Ra6  78.h6+  Kh7  79.Kd5  Draw.   See Ending 76. }
*

