{ Is it a good idea for White to play 64. Rf4+ followed by 65. Rg4 ? } 1. Rf4+
{ Exchanging rooks is the easiest way to win this game, provided one knows how to handle the doubled pawns.   64.h4 !  is another winning move. }
1... Ke5
{ 64...Ke3  is an attempt to counterattack, worthy of attention:  65.Rg4  Rh2  66.Kf1  Rxh3  67.g6  Rh8  68.g7  Rg8  69.Kg2  +- }
2. Rg4 Rxg4
{ Even though Black is lost no matter what he does, he could have put up more resistance by keeping the rooks on. After the exchange of rooks, the Black king needs four tempi to capture the g5-pawn; White's king, of course, is in time to defend it. }
3. hxg4 Ke6 4. Kf2 Kf7 5. Ke3 Kg7 6. Ke4
{ White must be careful to avoid the mutual zugzwang position:   69.Kf4 ?  Kg6  and a pawn goes west. }
6... Kg6 7. Kf4 Kf7 8. Kf5 Kg7 9. g6 Kh6 10. g7
{ Avoiding a last stalemate trick. } 10... Kxg7 11. Kg5 Kh7 12. Kf6 Kh6 13. g5+
Kh7 14. Kf7 Kh8 15. Kg6 Kg8 16. Kh6 { 1-0.  See Ending 77. } *

