[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/p4K2/P7/8/8/8/1k6/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Is White winning ? } 1. Ke6
{ White is winning in this famous position, often featured in the books to illustrate the king's ability to make dual-purpose moves. The position is from a real game which continued as follows. White's king needs five moves to capture the a-pawn, and one more move to get in Ka7-b7/8. He won't achieve this aim with just any route, though. He must choose the one route that simultaneously achieves a second objective, and that is to bodycheck the enemy king, preventing it from reaching the c7-square, before White can get his king to the b-file.   1.Ke7 ?  is wrong:  Kc3  2.Kd6  Kd4  3.Kc6  Ke5  4.Kb7  Kd6  5.Kxa7  Kc7 = }
1... Kc3 2. Kd5
{ 2.Kd6 ?  Suddenly, White strays from the ideal route, and by doing so lets victory slip through his hands.  Kd4  3.Kc6  Ke5  4.Kb7  Kd6  5.Kxa7  Kc7  Draw. }
2... Kd3 3. Kc6 Kd4 4. Kb7 Kd5 5. Kxa7
{ and the Black king is too late to imprison its colleague.  See Ending 80 & Ending 82. }
*

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
