{ Is f7-f8=Q winning here ? } 1. f8=Q
{ Queening the pawn leads to a theoretically drawn pawn endgame. By contrast, the knight and king can manoeuvre either to expel the bishop, or to block its influence over the f8-square.  There's no shortage of drama in the game of chess: White throws away the win at move 108! He should have played   108.Nc7 !  Kf4   108...Bg7  109.Ne8 !  Bh6  110.Nd6  followed by Nf5  109.Kf6  Kxg4   109...Bf8  110.Ne6+  110.Ne6  Kh5  111.Ng7+ }
1... Bxf8+ 2. Kxf8 { 109.Nxf8  Kf4  loses the last pawn. } 2... Kxe6 3. Kg7
{ White is going to win the g5-pawn, but the only thing Black needs to be able to do is answer Kxg5 with ...Kg7. }
3... Ke7
{ Of course, Black defends. Perhaps White expected the counterattack   110...Ke5 ??  111.Kg6 !  Kf4  112.Kh5  +- }
4. Kg6 Kf8 5. Kxg5 Kg7 6. Kh5 Kh7 7. g5 Kg7 8. g6 Kg8 9. Kg4 Kg7 10. Kg5 Kg8
{ Draw.  See Ending 80. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
