[White "Pawn Endings"]
[Black "Robert Kula vs. Zbignieuw Strzemiecki"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/4k3/8/3R1p1p/5Kb1/6P1/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Was White justified in playing 75. Rxf5 ? } 1. Rxf5
{ White is objectively winning. Simplification, however, is a blunder after which he loses all of his advantage. Now Black's defence is very simple.  After   75.Kg5 !  White wins by means of a typical procedure in this kind of positions: penetrate with the king via the dark squares, and put the opponent in zugzwang, e.g.  Kf7  76.Rd7+  and Black has a choice:  Ke6  (going to the back rank allows an even easier zugzwang)  77.Ra7  Ke5  78.Ra5+  Ke6  79.Rb5  Kd6   79...Kf7  80.Re5  +-  zugzwang  80.Kf6 !   80.Rxf5 ?  does not work here in view of  Bxf5  81.Kxf5  h4 !  The same motif as in the game.  80...Kd7  81.Rd5+  Ke8  after   81...Kc6  82.Rxf5  is winning  82.Re5+  Kd7  83.Re7+  Kd6  84.Ra7  Kd5  85.Ra4  Kd6  if   85...Bh3  86.Rf4  Bg4  87.Rxf5+ !  is winning  86.Rd4+  and 87. Rxg4 !, winning. }
1... Bxf5 2. Kxf5 h4
{ Draw. This is the only resource Black had to find: give up the pawn to bring about a change in the key squares or, as is the case here, convert it to a rook's pawn.  See Ending 81. }
*

