{ Can Back save the game ? } 1... Kxc5
{ Most players go out of their way to deny the opponent such a coveted commodity as a protected passed pawn. However, we see again that in chess there are no absolutes.  Black finds time to capture the c-pawn;   42...gxf4+ ?  helps White to improve his king:  43.Kxf4  Kxc5  44.Ke5 !  (kicking Black's king from the square of the g-pawn)  Kb4  45.g5  c5  46.g6  c4  47.g7  c3  48.g8=Q  c2  49.Qg5  +- }
2. f5 Kd6 3. Kf3 Kd5 4. Ke2 Kd6 5. Kd2 Ke5 6. Kc3 Kd5 7. Kd3 Ke5 8. Kc4 Kd6 9.
Kd4
{ Draw. White made no further progress and accepted a draw on move 67.  See Ending 89. }
