{ Choose between 54. Rxc2+ and 54. f4. } 1. Rxc2+
{ Here, this simplification is winning, the reason being the vis-a-vis of the kings. After   54.f4 ?  This position is all about choosing between two very similar pawn endings. Particularly, the position of the kings must be properly assessed.  b2 !  Whether or not this came as a surprise for White, the bottom line is that he is going to have to sacrifice his rook anyway. Note that Black's king is now more flexible, compared to the other variation.  55.Rxc2  Kb3  56.Rc6   56.Rxb2+  Kxb2  57.Kd3  Kc1 !  is similar. Black brings his king across in time to threaten White's last remaining pawn, once the latter plays Ke5.  56...b1=Q  57.Rb6+  Ka2  58.Rxb1  Kxb1  The Black king is much better here, and is fast enough to force the trade of pawns.  59.Kd3  Kc1  60.Ke2  Kc2  61.Ke3  Kd1  Draw. }
1... bxc2 2. Kxc2 { White's king corrals its counterpart in the corner: } 2...
Ka1 { 55...e5  56.Kd3  Kb2  57.Ke4  +- } 3. f4 Ka2 4. Kc3 Kb1 5. Kd4 Kc2 6. Ke5
{ See Ending 80. } *

