{ Does it make any difference whether White plays 39. Rxg2 or 39. Kxh3 ? } 1.
Kxh3
{ There is one tiny difference between 39. Rxg2 and 39. Kxh3, namely the position of the king. This nuance is enough to alter the evaluation of the position. This move wins two tempi compared to the game. In addition, thanks to some dual-purpose play, White's king is in time to enter the square:   39.Rxg2 ?  Losing, as the king won't be able to step into the square of the black a-pawn.  hxg2  40.Kxg2  a5  0-1. }
1... Rxg4 2. Kxg4 a5 3. Kf5 { White already threatens to promote the pawn. }
3... Kc6 { 41...Kc7  lets White's king into the square:  42.Ke5 = } 4. e5
{ It's necessary to keep playing dual-purpose moves. Stepping into the square straight away fails:   42.Ke5 ?  Kc5 !  body-checks and wins }
4... a4 { 42...Kd7  again lets the White king into the square:  43.Ke4 } 5. e6
a3 { The Black pawn will promote, but the careful } 6. Kg6
{ sidestepping promotion with check, makes sure the White pawn will promote, too.  See Ending 82. }
