{ Is trading rooks a correct idea for White ? } 1. Rxc6+
{ Trading rooks is correct, provided one really understands the reason why !  Objectively correct yet dubious, since less risky alternatives were available, such as   57.Re8 !  which should hold, e.g.  Kb5   57...Rh6  58.Rc8+  58.Kd4  c3  59.Re5+  Kb6  60.Re1  c2  61.Rc1 = }
1... Kxc6 2. Kd4 Kb5 3. Ke3
{ 59.Kc3 ??  If White had intended this, he would have been far better off keeping the rooks on.  Kc5  60.Kc2  Kd4  61.Kd2  c3+  62.Kc1  We have reached a basic theoretical position. Black wins by means of triangulation.  Kd5 !  63.Kd1  Kc5 !  After the last two moves, which White can't copycat, Black's king is ready to seize the corresponding square: if White's king goes to c2, Black's will go to c4; if White goes Kc1, Black will answer with ...Kd4.  64.Kc1  Kd4 !  65.Kc2  Kc4 !  66.Kc1  Kb3  67.Kb1  Kxa3  68.Kc2  Kb4  69.Kc1  Kb3  70.Kb1  c2+  71.Kc1  a3  0-1. }
3... Kc5 4. Ke4 { and Black can make no progress whatsoever.  See Ending 86. }
