{ Can White win the game ? } 1. h4
{ White wins, but theoretical knowledge of the endgame rook's and knight's pawn vs. rook's pawn, as well as bishop's and rook's pawn vs. rook's pawn, is required. Let's see why the other two natural options fail:   60.fxg6 ?  Kxg6  61.h4  Kf6 =  the opposition saves Black.   60.Ke4 ?  expecting Black to exchange, is wrong because  h5 !  quickly leads to a king and pawn vs. king endgame. }
1... gxf5
{ The waiting strategy   60...Kf7  61.fxg6+  Kxg6  62.Ke5  leads to a different version of the endgame knight's and rook's pawn vs. rook's pawn, in which White outflanks her opponent after  Kg7  63.Ke6  Kg6  64.h5+ !  Kg5  65.Kf7  Kxg4  66.Kg6  +- }
2. gxf5 Kf7 3. Ke5 Ke7 4. f6+ Kf8 5. Kf5 Kf7 6. h5
{ This is essentially the same position as in the previous exercise, where the attacker won by triangulation. }
6... Kf8 7. Kg6 { 1-0.  See Ending 86 & Ending 87. } *

