{ Black has four moves. Which ones lead to a draw, and which ones lead to defeat ? }
1... Ke8
{ Black must keep the rook's pawn on his second rank and cede the opposition to White when there is an odd number of squares between the latter's pawns. By contrast, if the number of squares is even, he should keep the opposition. Specifically, with a White pawn on h3 or h5, Black should play ...Ke8; with a pawn on h4 Black should play ...Kf8; with a pawn on h2, which may still choose to advance one or two squares, Black is simply lost. I have provided an example of each of the four possible moves in this position. It's funny to analyse some database statistics behind this simple position, particularly in terms of result and Elo rating. Seeing how many errors occur in such a simple position never ceases to amaze me.   77...Kf8 ?  is the most frequently played move, seen seven times in my database, with a disheartening 0% score.  78.Ke6  Ke8  79.f7+  Kf8  80.Kf6  1-0 Janyska-Chudinovskih, Olomouc 2011.   77...h6 ?  (move 61 in this game)  leads to a triangulation endgame:  78.Ke5  Kf8  79.Kf4  Kg8  80.Ke4  Kf8  81.Ke5  1-0 Jamroz-Szilagyi, Lublin 1968.   77...Kg8 !  also leads to a draw, for the same reasons as ...Ke8, but most players reject this move, most probably for psychological reasons.  78.Ke6  Kf8  79.Ke5  Kf7  80.Kf5  Draw, Balogh-Csema, Hungary tt 2008/09. }
2. Kf4 Kf7 3. Kg5 Ke6 { Draw.  See Ending 85 & Ending 86. } *

