{ Was it a good idea for White to play 68. Rxf6 ? } 1. Rxf6
{ At first glance, it might seem like a good idea to eliminate a pawn with 68. Rxf6. Furthermore, it looks as if the other pawn might drop soon, too. But upon closer scrutiny, this variation actually gives Black a theoretically winning endgame.   68.Kf1 !  attacking the c4-pawn, engineers an immediate draw. }
1... Nf4 2. Rxf4+ Kxf4 3. Ke1
{ It looks as if the other pawn will be lost too, but: } 3... Rxe2+ 4. Kxe2 Ke4
{ Black will now use basic outflanking technique to capture White's pawn, and gain control of a key square. After that, it's time to shepherd his own pawn down to greener pastures. }
5. Kd2 Kf3 6. Kd1 Ke3 7. Kc2 Ke2 8. Kc1 Kd3 9. Kb2 Kd2 10. Kb1 Kxc3 11. Kc1 Kd3
12. Kd1 c3 13. Kc1 c2 { 0-1.  See Ending 79. } *

