{ White captured the h4-pawn. Was that a good decision ? } 1. Nxh4
{ Capturing the h4-pawn was a howler. Most likely, the White player underestimated the pent-up power of Black's queenside pawns to engineer a successful breakthrough. Had he kept the knight on the board, the game would still have been within drawing margins.   56.Kg2  is one possibility to keep equality;   56.Nxg5 ?  is another way to go awry:  fxg5  and the same breakthrough as in the game can't be prevented. White's protected passed pawn is an asset of very limited value. }
1... Bxh4 2. Kxh4
{ Doubled pawn constellations are vulnerable to breakthroughs, and the technique to pull one off is to sacrifice either one or several pawns. }
2... c4 { Ensures the promotion of a black pawn. } 3. Kh5
{ 58.bxc4  a4  followed by ...a4-a3.   58.dxc4  a4  59.bxa4  b3  60.cxb3  d3  -+ }
3... a4 4. Kg6 { 59.bxa4  b3 !  -+ } 4... axb3 5. cxb3 cxd3 6. Kg7 d2 7. g5
d1=Q 8. gxf6+ Kd6 9. f7 Qg4+ 10. Kf6 Qh4+ { 0-1.  See Ending 92. } *

