{ White played 50. g4. Was that a good decision ? } 1. g4
{ The move g3-g4 was wrong: White could have won by keeping the rooks on. Simplification, by contrast, leads to a difficult yet drawn pawn endgame with a subtle king manoeuvre at the end of a long variation.   50.Ra4 !  g4  51.Ra8+  Kxc7  52.Rh8  among other options, should be winning. }
1... dxc4 { 50...Rxf4 ?  51.Rxf4  gxf4  52.h4  +-  would be elementary. } 2.
gxf5 Kxc7 3. Kd2
{ leads to a surprising variation:   52.fxg5  fxg5  53.Kd2  Kd6  54.Ke3  Ke5  55.f6 !?  Since the e5-pawn is lost anyway, White might as well create some room to get to grips with the c4-pawn.  Kxf6  56.Kd4  Kf5  57.Kxc4  Kg4  The crucial moment: White's king has a choice of moves.  58.Kb5   58.Kd5 !?  is the most logical try, but it's not winning. Let's find out why:  Kh3  59.Ke5  Kxh2  60.Kf5  Once again, we reach this typical paradox of a king using dual-purpose moves to achieve its goal.  Kg3 !  61.c4   61.Kxg5  Kf3  and the white pawn drops  61...Kf3  and White has a choice between allowing a position with bare kings, or one with king-and-queen vs. king-and-queen.  58...Kh3  59.c4  Kxh2  60.c5  Draw. }
3... gxf4 { Allowing an outside pawn. } 4. Ke2
{ 53.h4  Kd6  54.h5  Ke7  55.Ke2  Kf7  56.Kf3  Kg7  57.Kxf4  Kh6  58.Kg4  Kh7  and there is no way to make any progress. }
4... Kd6 5. Kf3 Ke5 6. h3 Kxf5 7. h4 Ke5 8. h5 Kf5 9. h6 Kg6 10. Kxf4 Kxh6 11.
