[SetUp "1"]

{ Is simplifying by means of 57. f6 winning for White ? } 1. Nf4
{ White probably didn't expect too much from this position. Presumably, he didn't believe his position could be improved by doing some manoeuvring. This assumption, however, was wrong, as there is in fact a quick and efficient way to win the game by force.   57.f6 ?  Simplification leads to a version of the endgame with blocked rook's pawns and a passed pawn on the other flank, which in this case is only a draw. This would be different if the pawn stood on h2.  Bxf6  58.Nxf6  Kxf6  59.Kxh4  Kf5 !  Necessary to prevent the variation   59...Kg6  60.Kg4  +-  Now White, perhaps disheartened at this point, rejects the natural continuation with 60. Kg3  (which would promise no more than a draw in any case)  and keeps on playing without much point.  60.Kh5  The natural continuation would be   60.Kg3  Kg5   For natural positions such as in the diagram, the two lines allow us to come to a conclusion regarding the result of the game: the longest  (dotted)  line from h3 to c8  (the route of the Black king after capturing the White pawn)  reaches the c-file above the shorter line a5-c7  (the route of the White king after capturing the Black pawn)  and therefore the result is a draw. Note that if the h-pawn were further back  (in this case, on h2)  White would be winning.  61.Kf3  Kh4  62.Ke4  Kxh3  63.Kd5  Kg4  64.Kc5  Kf5  65.Kb5  Ke6  66.Kxa5  Kd7  67.Kb6  Kc8 =  The Black king is just in time.  60...Kf4  61.h4  Kf5  62.Kh6  Kf6  63.Kh7  Kf7  64.h5  Kf6  65.Kh6  Kf7  66.Kg5  Kg7  67.Kf5  Kh6  68.Ke5  Kxh5  69.Kd5  Kg6  70.Kc5  Kf7  71.Kb5  Ke7  72.Kxa5  Kd7  73.Kb6  Kc8  74.a5  Kb8  75.a6  Ka8  76.a7  Draw. }
1... Bf6 2. Ng6+ Ke4 3. Nf8
{ 59.Nxh4  Bxh4  60.Kxh4  Kxf5  61.Kg3  Kg5  is the same endgame as in the game, within drawing margins }
3... Bd8 4. Nd7 Kd5
{ Now White's king is able to support its pawn from the g6-square, which makes all the difference: }
5. Kh5 Kd6 6. Nf8 Ke5 { 62...Be7  63.Ne6 } 7. Kg6
{ Followed by advancing the f-pawn, will cost Black a piece.  See Ending 83. }
*

