{ Choose between 49... Kd6 and 49... Na3. } 1... Na3+
{ Denying White the possibility of forcing the trade of this piece, for example:   49...Kd6 ?  The key to a White victory is to force an exchange of the badly placed knight without altering the pawn structure. That is why Black should avoid such a scenario from his very first move. This move leads to a losing endgame, handled by White in impeccable style, helped perhaps by home analysis over adjournment.  50.Kd4 !  The beginning of an impressive technical performance by Nikolaevsky. The first stage of his plan is to trap the black knight in such a way that it will be forced to exchange itself for the bishop. Not only does   50.Kd4  prepare to control the b5-square with the bishop, it also anticipates any attempts by Black to prolong the life of his knight via the d2-square.  50...Na3   50...Nd2  51.Kd3  Nb1  52.Bf1  Na3  53.Kd2  traps the knight in similar fashion to the game.  51.Bf1  Nc2+  52.Kd3  Ne1+  53.Ke2  Nc2  54.Kd2  Na3  55.Kc1 !  A paradox: White's king returns to the back rank to trap the knight.  Kc5  56.Kb2  Nc4+  57.Bxc4  Kxc4  58.Kc2  Finally, the minor pieces have come off. We have now reached an endgame with bishop's and rook's pawn vs. rook's pawn. The White a-pawn being on the second rank always guarantees victory, as explained in Exercise 218.  Kc5  59.Kd3  Kd5  60.c4+  Kc5  61.Kc3  Kc6  62.Kd4  Kd6  63.c5+  Kc6  64.Kc4  Kc7  65.Kd5  Kd7  66.c6+  Kc7  67.Kc5  Kc8  68.Kd6  Kd8  69.c7+  Kc8  70.Kc6  1-0. }
2. Kb4 { 50.Kd3  Nb5 = } 2... Nc2+
{ followed by ...Ne3.  See Ending 85.
See Exercise 218. } *

