{ Is simplification beginning with 43... Nhxf3 correct ? } 1... Nhxf3
{ Don't let appearances fool you - this exercise is not identical to the previous one. Immediate simplification is a mistake here. }
2. Ra4
{ to prevent ...f5-f4. The knights remain awkwardly placed, unable to save the f5-pawn.   44.Rxf3 ?  White, the lower rated player, immediately returns the favour by making the typical beginner's mistake of capturing pieces at the first opportunity given. Having said that, the defence is by no means easy and requires great precision.  Nxf3  45.Kxf3  f4  46.Kf2  Kf5  We have reached a position seen in Exercises 218 & 258.  47.Kf3  Ke5  48.Kf2  Ke4  49.Ke2  f3+  50.Kf2  Kf4  51.Kf1  Ke3  52.Ke1  f2+  53.Kf1  Kf3  The crucial moment: White must move his pawn, allowing Black to react accordingly.  54.h3  Kg3  55.h4  h5  0-1. }
2... Nd2
{ 44...Kf6  45.Ra6+  Kg7  46.Kf4  also here, Black loses the f5-pawn.   44...h5  45.h4+ ! = }
3. h4+ Kh5 4. Kf4 { Black loses the f5-pawn.  See Ending 85. } *

