{ Choose between 45. Kd2, 45. Kc2 and 45. Kb4. } 1. Kc2
{ In this common and dangerous pawn structure, White faces a tough decision: should he risk a decisive breakthrough by moving away from the pawn cluster, or should he go for passive defence, allowing the enemy king in ? White's position, however, is not beyond salvation, as he has a crucial game-saving resource at his disposal, based on mutual zugzwang. This surprising move keeps the balance.  Surely, White understood that   45.Kb4 ?  would take the king too far away from the action, allowing the breakthrough  f4 !  46.exf4   46.Kc3  f3  47.gxf3  exf3  48.Kd2  g4  -+  46...gxf4  47.Kc3  Kc5  48.Kd2  Kd4  49.Ke2  e3  50.fxe3+  fxe3  -+   45.Kd2 ?  Kc4  46.Ke2  Kc3 ?  Black misses his chance:   46...g4 !  47.Kd2  Kb3  48.Kd1  Kc3  49.Ke2  Kc2  50.Ke1  Kd3  51.Kf1  g3 !  52.fxg3  hxg3  53.h4  Kd2  54.h5  f4  and the black pawn queens with mate.  47.g3 !  Kc2  48.gxh4  gxh4  49.Ke1  Kc3  Draw. }
1... Kc4
{ 45...f4  doesn't work with the king one square closer:  46.Kd2  f3  47.g4 !  not   47.g3 ?  g4 !  -+  47...hxg3  48.fxg3 = }
2. Kd2
{ Reaching an unusual zugzwang position: Black has no useful move in this exact same position as in the game but with Black himself to move. Let's look at the options: }
2... f4
{ 46...g4  47.Kc2  Now, Black has no reserve tempi available and can't win back the opposition either.   46...Kb3  47.Ke2  Kc2   47...g4  48.Ke1  leads to the same position  48.g3 !  Kc1  49.gxh4  gxh4  50.Ke1  and with this pawn structure, Black's king can't outflank its antagonist, not even by means of the opposition. }
3. Ke2
{ followed by f2-f3 with equality  (if Black insists in bringing his king in, he even ends up losing)  .  See Ending 92. }
