{ Black has a comfortably winning game. Can you guess what happened after the reckless move 35... h6 ? }
1... h6
{ Black has, believe it or not, eleven winning moves in this position ! Evidently, he wasn't familiar with the bizarre zugzwang position shown in the diagram below, and which has become a standard example in manuals of endgame theory.  A blunder that brings Black to the very edge of defeat, with only one way to save the game.   35...Bf5  was possibly the easiest solution. }
2. Nxe4 dxe4
{ 36...hxg5 !  was the only move to draw, but who would make such a move after 35... h6 ?  37.Nxg5+  Kf6  with a likely draw. }
3. gxh6 Kf6 4. h5
{ Black falls into one of the most painful zugzwang positions known to man, even though it is more common to see this happen with a white pawn on f5 rather than h5. If he moves the king, the h6-pawn will be unstoppable. By contrast, White's king is perfectly capable of dealing with the three black pawns, and therefore it is clear that Black can only postpone the inevitable. }
4... e3+ 5. Kf3 e4+ 6. Ke2
{ Now we witness how the Black pawns will drop, one after the other. At this point, Black really could have resigned with a clear conscience, but he couldn't bring himself to do it, and played on out of spite. }
6... Kg5 7. h7 Kf4 8. h8=Q d3+ 9. Ke1 f5 10. Qf6 e2 11. Qd4 Kf3 12. h6 f4 13.
Qf2+ Kg4 14. Qg2+ Kf5 15. Qh3+ Kg5 16. h7 f3 17. Qg3+ { 1-0. } *

