{ White spent twenty-three moves to get to this position, but he can still win. How should he continue ? }
1. Ke3
{ 97.Be5 !?  This position is where White often throws away the win. Memorizing one concrete mechanism is almost mandatory. The player handling the White pieces in this game wasn't familiar with it. This move, while not an error  (it leads to mate in the same number of moves as via the prescribed method)  is a step in the wrong direction.  Kc2  98.Ke3 ?  White mixes up ideas and, by doing so, compromises the win: he has spent twenty-five moves, and will need twenty-one more with perfect play to give checkmate.   98.Nc5 !  creates a second triangle, as published by Deletang as far back as 1923, to keep the king bound. The key point to remember is that the a3-square is not really a hole in this bishop-knight wall that the Black king can exploit: once the it reaches a3, White answers Bc3, blocking the b4-exit.  98...Kb3  99.Bd4  Kc4  100.Nc3  Kb4  101.Kd3 ?  The decisive error because of the fifty-move rule: White has used twenty-seven moves, and now he needs twenty-four more. By contrast,   101.Ke4 !  leads to mate in 'only' twenty.  101...Ka5  102.Kc4  Ka6  103.Kc5  Kb7  104.Nd5  Kc8  105.Kd6  Kb7  106.Bc3  Ka6  107.Kc6  Ka7  108.Nb6  Ka6  109.Bb4  Ka7  110.Bd6  Ka6  111.Bb8  Ka5  112.Nd5  Ka4  113.Kc5  Kb3  114.Nb4  Kc3  115.Bf4 !  One has to feel sympathy for this effort. Finally, White has found the winning mechanism, which he executes with great precision, but he does so only eight moves before reaching the fifty-move limit. If there were such a thing as poetic justice on the chessboard, it would dictate that White deserves the win, but alas, the chess rules are governed by the maxim dura lex, sed lex  (a tough law, but the law nonetheless)  .  Kb3  116.Be5  Ka4  117.Kc4  Ka5  118.Bc7+  Ka4  119.Nd3  Ka3  120.Bb6  Ka4  121.Nb2+  Ka3  122.Kc3  Ka2  123.Bc5  Draw. Drawn because of the fifty-move rule. White would have given checkmate in five more moves. Remember what I said in the introduction: learn from other people's mistakes... }
1... Kc2 2. Nd2 Kc3 { and now } 3. Bd6
{ The Black king is locked in a cage it can't escape from. The rest of the winning procedure is child's play.  See Ending 93. }
