{ The bishop has only two moves to draw. Which ones? } 1... Bg3
{ Keeps the bishop on the right diagonal.   91...Bc7 ?  loses on the spot to  92.Rh8+  +-  ;   91...Bb8 ?  also loses on account of  92.Rh8+  Kc7  93.Rxb8 !  The two moves that draw are the ones that keep the bishop on the h2-b8 diagonal without disturbing the Black king's mobility.   91...Bc1 ?  Losing, as the bishop won't be able to get back onto the right diagonal. Let's look at all the other moves:  92.Ra4  Kc7  93.Ra7+  Kc8  94.Rf7  Bd2  95.Rh7 ??  So far the bishop has not been able to reach the right diagonal. The win would be in doubt if the black bishop reaches a5, but the winning procedure was as follows:   95.c7  Kb7  96.Kd6  Ba5  and now White wins like in Exercise 272, De Firmian-Georgiev:  97.Rg7  with zugzwang  or   97.Rh7  or even   97.Re7  97...Bb6  98.c8=Q+  Kxc8  99.Kc6  +-  95...Be3+  96.Kd5  Bf4  97.Rh3  Bg5 ??  1-0. Again the bishop leaves the right diagonal - Black's position is again losing, although it is slightly strange to resign at this point already.   91...Be5 !  is also correct for the same reason;   91...Be3+ ?  92.Kd6  +-   See Ending 97. }
