{ Can Black make a draw ? } 1... h6
{ This is not a fortress, since White's king can penetrate via the dark squares. Black, however, does have the possibility to give up the pawn and force a transition into a position with a rook's pawn on the sixth rank, as seen in the previous exercise. It is not the only move, but a unique idea nonetheless. Black must give up his h-pawn, transposing to a well-known theoretical draw.   57...Bc2  58.Ke5  already would leave Black with an only move:  h6 !  . }
2. gxh6 Kh8 3. Kg5 Bd3
{ 59...Bh7 ??  Is it ever too late to spoil a perfectly good position ? The bishop needed to move further away.  60.Ra8+ !  Bg8  Now that the bishop has been forced off the diagonal, White can win by force by bringing the h-pawn to the seventh rank.  61.Rc8  Kh7  62.Rc7+  Kh8  63.Kg6  Bd5  64.h7  Be4+  65.Kh6  Bf5  66.Rf7  1-0. }
