{ Is it a fortress? } 1. Rxg4
{ The move Rxg4 loses as it leads to a position with a knight's pawn on the third rank: the king won't be able to remain behind the pawn.   49.Rf8  is objectively more resilient, but also loses:  Ke4  50.Re8+   50.Rf4+  Kd3  51.Rxg4  transposes to the game  50...Kf3  51.Rf8+  Ke2  52.Re8+  Kxf2  53.Rf8+  Ke2  54.Rxf1  Kxf1  -+  reaching a well-known endgame with blocked pawns. }
1... Qxf2+ 2. Kh3 Qf5
{ This is fastest, but there are a few alternatives that win, too. The key is not to let White's king tuck itself away behind the pawn; for example   50...Qe2 ?  allows a draw:  51.Rf4 !  and it won't be possible any longer to prevent White's king from reaching g2, provided White is careful and keeps his rook on the f-file, e.g.  Kd5   51...Qe1  52.Kg2 =  is much easier  52.Rf5+  Ke4  53.Rf4+  Ke5  Semi-zugzwang  (i.e. a non-decisive zugzwang position)  .  54.Rf8 ! = }
3. Kh4 Qh7+ 4. Kg5 Qh3
{ The pawn drops.   The game continued:  52...Qf5  53.Kh4  Qh7  54.Kg5  Qh3  See Ending 79 & Ending 99. }
