1... Rg7
{ was correct and in accordance with the suggested method of defence. White could win this position if his king is allowed to the eighth rank and not subject to immediate eviction. That is why the best defensive formula for Black is to place the rook on the g7-square, unless this would be clearly wrong for tactical reasons.   59...Kg8 ??  Losing, as it allows the white king to march onto the eighth rank. The winning procedure is by no means easy, but Karpov never gives his prey a chance to escape.  60.Qe6+  Kh7  61.Kf7   61.Ke7  Rg8  62.Kf7  Rg7+  63.Kf8  was a few moves quicker.  61...Rg8  62.Qf5+  Kh8  63.Qe5+  Kh7  1-0. There might have followed  64.Qe6  Rg5  65.Kf8  and the king reaches the eighth rank without the opponent being able to immediately expel it. Now the rook loses its pawn support and is lost:  Rg1   65...h5  66.Qf6  Rg6  67.Qe5  Kh6  68.Kf7  h4  69.Qe4  Rg5  70.Qxh4+  Rh5  71.Qf6+  66.Qe4+  Kh8  67.Qd4+  +-  also good were   59...Rg1  and   59...Rg8 }
