{ Does it make any difference where Black's king goes to ? } 1... Kh5
{ Black gets an easily winning game if he moves his king to the h-file. This is, however, a paradoxical decision, and one of the least well-known in endgame theory.   73...Kf5 ?  74.Kh2  Qb2+  75.Kg1  Qe2  76.Kh1  Qf2  77.Rg2 !  Qe1+  78.Rg1  Qe4+  79.Kh2  Qc2+  80.Rg2  Qc7+  81.Kh1  Kf4  82.Kh2 !?  White could move the rook, but keeping it on the g2-square is easiest  (provided this is not immediately losing)  .  Kf3+  83.Kh1  Qc1+  84.Kh2  Qe1  Now there is no choice but to move the rook, which might as well attack the queen.  85.Rg1  Qe2+  This move prevents the rook from going back to g2, but now there is no way to get the king in.  86.Kh1  Qe4  87.Rg2 !  White doesn't think twice to put the rook back on the g2-square. Strictly speaking, the move wasn't entirely forced, as   87.Rg4  was another possibility. For the remainder of the game, David Navara demonstrates flawless defence.  87...Qe5  88.Kg1  Qe1+  89.Kh2  Qh4  Again the rook must move, and the most useful thing is to attack the queen with it.  90.Rg4  Qf2+  91.Kh1  Qe1+  92.Kh2  Qe5+  93.Kh1  Qe1+  94.Kh2  Draw. }
