{ Can Black save the game ? } 1... Nce7
{ The ideal defensive set-up is to have the knights as close to the king as possible, but not mutually defending one another. Here, Black is not yet ideally coordinated, but with precise play he can hold a draw. This move, while also temporarily creating a position with mutually protecting knights, achieves the ideal set-up:   89...Ncd4 ?  Black fails to head for the defensive formation described above. The set-up with two knights defending one another is much less effective, as the enemy king is able to infiltrate, after which zugzwang threats will be hanging over the defender's head.  90.Qc5+  Kf4  91.Qc7+  Kf3  92.Qe5  Kg4  93.Ke4  The king slips through between the two equestrians.  Kg5  94.Qf4+  Kf6  95.Qg4  Kf7  96.Ke5  Now White chases the enemy king toward the edge of the board.  Ke7  97.Qg8  Kd7  98.Qd5+  Kc7  99.Qc5+  Kd7  100.Kd5  Kd8  101.Qa7 ?!  Visually impressive, as Black's king is on the rim, but the Nalimov tablebases show that with this move, White unduly lengthens the road to victory: it is now mate in thirty-nine moves, whereas   101.Qb6+  would lead to mate in fifteen.  101...Kc8   101...Ne7+  102.Ke4  Ndc6  103.Qb7  +-  102.Qf7 !  1-0. The final position is self-explanatory: one knight must move, leading to the loss of both. }
2. Qc5+ Ke6 3. Ke4 Nd6+ 4. Kf4 Nd5+
{ 92...Nf7  93.Qc4+  Kf6  94.Qa6+  Kg7  95.Qb7  Kf8  96.Qd7  +-  Zugzwang. } 5.
Kg4
{ . Black has achieved the ideal set-up. Observe the almost impenetrable wall created by the knights. Let's try a variation in which White's king manages to slip through the only weak spot in the wall: }
5... Ke5 6. Kf3 Ke6 7. Ke2 Ke5 8. Kd3 Ke6 9. Qa5
{ 97.Kd4  Nf5+  and the white king must go back } 9... Nf4+ 10. Kd4 Ne2+ 11.
Kd3 Nf4+ { and the knights get back into the ideal defensive set-up. } *

