{ Choose between 54. Bd5 and 54. Ba8. } 1. Ba8
{ White has the very common F14 fortress, which has saved many players half a point. Black's king, however, is ideally placed, so the defender must play very accurately. This move was necessary to avoid the rook coming to g5 with gain of tempo:   54.Bd5 ?  Losing.  Rb5 !   54...g5 ?  looks totally forced, but is wrong, since after  55.hxg5  Rb5  the bishop is on a better square:  56.Bc6   56.Be6  is also a draw with a similar variation  56...Rxg5  57.Kh2  Kf2  58.Kh3  Rxg3+  59.Kh4 =  55.Bc6   55.Be6 !?  is also a rather tough nut to crack. Let's see one variation:  Rb1+  56.Kh2  Rb2+  57.Kh3   57.Kg1  Kf3  -+  57...Kf2  58.Kh2   58.Bd5  Rd2  59.Be4  Rd1  60.Kh2  Rg1  61.Bxg6  Rg2+ !  -+  58...Re2  59.Bf7  Kf1+  60.Kh1  Rg2  61.Bxg6  Rxg3  -+  55...Rc5  56.Bb7  g5  57.hxg5  Rxg5   It's interesting to compare and contrast this diagram to the variations above: the difference in bishop placement is decisive. Concretely, White makes a draw if the bishop is on one of the highlighted squares, always by means of the only move  Kh2.  By contrast, with the pawn on  h6  , White makes a draw with the bishop on any logical square, including  b7  ,  c8  and  a8  , but only with the move Kg2:  see Exercise 285.  58.Kg2 ?!   58.Kh2 !?  is much more stubborn, though losing too, as seen in the following games:  Kf2  59.Kh3  Rxg3+  60.Kh4  and now only one rook move wins:  Rg7 !   60...Rb3 ?  61.Bc6 =  Rc3  62.Be8  Kf3  63.Kxh5  Rc8  64.Bf7  Kf4  65.Kg6  Rc6+  66.Kg7  Kg5  67.Kh8  Rh6+  68.Kg7  Rd6  69.Kh8  Kf6  70.Bg8  Rd8  71.Kh7  Draw in Nihal-Subramaniam, Bhubaneswar 2016.  61.Bc6  Rh7  62.Bd5   62.Be8  Kf3  63.Bxh5+  Kf4  -+  62...Ke3  63.Bg8  Rh8  64.Bf7  Kf4  65.Bb3  Rb8  66.Bc4  Rc8  67.Be6  Rc6  68.Bd5  Rc2  69.Kh3  Kg5  0-1 Ftacnik-Murey, New York 1987.  58...h4  59.Kh3  hxg3  60.Kg2  Kf4  61.Bc6  Rc5  62.Bb7  Rc2+  63.Kg1  g2  64.Kh2  Rb2  0-1. }
1... Rb5 2. Kg2 g5 3. hxg5 Rxg5 4. Kh3 Rg4 5. Bc6 Kf2 6. Bd5 Rxg3+ 7. Kh4
{ The position is a draw: White can simply capture the h5-pawn. It's important to note that this position is a draw with the bishop on d5 or c6, but if it is on b7 or a8 the position is losing. We will see this in a couple of variations later.  See the F14 Fortress. }
*

