{ Can Black win the game ? } 1... Bxd4+
{ Black must choose either to defend his pawn or allow White to head for a well-known fortress. With accurate play, however, Black can prevent this.   41...Rc8 ?!  is too passive, and shouldn't be enough for a win. If Black wasn't sure how to avoid the fortress, perhaps this was the best practical try, though. }
2. Bxd4+ Rxd4 3. Bxc6
{ The critical moment. I imagine that Black lacked detailed knowledge of the fortress F16, or else he would have taken appropriate measures. }
3... h5
{ prevents the creation of F16 and wins easily:   43...Rd2 ?  44.Bf3 !  Kg7  45.h4 ??  White's joy is short-lived, as he immediately self-destructs his fortress. The position with the black pawn on h6 allows an easy defence for White: he simply has to prevent the move ...h6-h5 with his bishop. With the black pawn on h7 the possible 'fortress' created doubts for a long time: since Black can carry through ...h7-h5 with the help of either his rook or his king, and in general this should be winning. However, here White can keep the draw. The key is twofold: he has to prevent the ...h7-h5 advance until the black rook leaves the second rank, and then has to meet the advance to h5 with the immediate response h3-h4 !. And this should happen under two conditions: the bishop stays on the long diagonal in case of the advance ...g5-g4, and the pawn on h4 is not lost in case of a pawn exchange. or example:   45.Bh5 !  (White has to prevent ...h7-h5 for as long as he can, and force Black to use both his pieces to support the push)  Kh6  46.Bf3  Kg6  47.Be4+  Kg7  48.Bf3  Rb2   48...h6 ?  49.Bh5 !  is much easier for White, who can move his king while the black king is away, and move his bishop to and fro between g4 and h5 when the black king reaches e3  49.Bh5  Kh6  50.Bf3  Rb4 !?  (controlling the check on e4)  51.Bc6  Kg7  52.Be8  Re4  53.Bh5  Re7  54.Bf3  Kg6  55.Kg2  h5  56.h4 !  g4   56...gxh4  57.gxh4  leads to a simple position, because one of Black's two pieces has to keep defending the pawn on h5  57.Bc6 =  . We have reached a lesser-known and less frequent version of fortress F14.  45...gxh4  46.gxh4  Kf6  47.Be4  h5  48.Bf3  Kg6  0-1. The h4-pawn will drop in a few moves' time. }
4. Kf2 h4 5. gxh4 gxh4 6. Ke3 Rb4
{ followed by penetrating with the king via the dark squares. } *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
