{ Choose between 74. Ke4, 74. Kd4 and 74. h7+. } 1. Ke4
{ is the correct move and, with the king one square closer, it's already possible to trade the pawns for the rook by means of h6-h7+, for example:   74.Kd4 ?  is also a draw after  Rg1 ! =  . The pawns are blocked, and the rook can be put on g6 and then on c6. A careless player however could still fall for  75.g7  Rxg7 ??  - in this case, Black's king wouldn't be able to hide inside the fortress.   74.h7+ ?  Finding White's only winning move is easy: all he has to be wary of is Black creating a fortress with blocked b-pawns on the queenside.  Kg7 !!  Perhaps this is what White had missed. Black gains two tempi to bring in the king.  If   74...Rxh7 ?  75.gxh7+  Kxh7  76.Ke4  Kg6  77.Kd5  Kf7  78.Kd6  Ke8  79.Kc7  +-  , calling off any construction work.  75.Bc3+  Kxg6  76.h8=Q  Rxh8  77.Bxh8  Kf7  78.Kd4  Ke6  Draw. The king hides on a8/b8, and no force in the world can do anything against it. }
1... Rh5 2. Bf4
{ Not yet   75.h7+  Kg7 !  76.Bc3+  Kxg6  77.h8=N+  Rxh8  78.Bxh8  Kf7  and the king makes it in time. }
2... Kh8 3. Be5+ Kg8 4. h7+ Rxh7 5. gxh7+ Kxh7 6. Kd5 *

