{ Does it matter where Black's king moves to ? } 1... Kd2
{ perhaps counter-intuitive: the Black king should go to the corner where the knight is, and circle around the pawn. For example:   65...Kb2 ?  Black had better choose a careful move. Although the g-pawn has crossed Troitsky's line, Black can still lose if he lets his king get trapped in a corner that the blockading knight can reach.  66.Kb4 !  Now the king is going to be trapped in the corner. That being so, executing the winning plan is so difficult that few players manage to play flawlessly.  Ka2  67.Ndf5 ?  This lets the king escape; an understandable error, especially given that this was a rapid game.   67.Nb5 !  was the correct move.  67...Kb2 ?   67...Kb1  68.Nd4  Kb2 =  .  68.Ne3  Ka2  69.Nc4  Kb1  70.Kc3  Kc1  71.Nb2  Kb1  72.Nd3  Ka1  73.Kb3  Now the king is completely trapped and can only move back and forth in a tiny little cage on the a1-/b1-squares. The time has come for the blockading knight to take an active role as fellow-executioner of Black's royal highness.  Kb1  74.Ne2  1-0. }
2. Nb3+ Ke1 3. Nc5 Kf2 4. Nce4+ Ke3 5. Kc3 Kf3 6. Kd3 Kg2 7. Ke2 Kh3 8. Kf1 Kh2
9. Kf2 Kh3 10. Kg1 Kh4 11. Kg2 { , stalemate. } *

