{ The Black pawns look extremely dangerous. Many live spectators, however, saw that White could save the game. How ? By looking at the engine evaluation, of course ! }
1. Nb3
{ is a move well worthy of a diagram: The knight controls the queening square and allows the White king to quickly get into the corner. A perfectly logical move, therefore, once you understand that capturing the knight leads to an elementary fortress:   74.Ke2 ?  White opts for the natural move. The spectacular move that saves the game will come as a complete surprise - unless you've been keeping an eye out for a fortress.  a3 !  Carlsen is famous for not letting his opponents off the hook. The a-pawn will prove too strong.  75.bxa3  bxa3  76.Kd1  a2  77.Nb3  Kc4  78.Na1  Kc3  79.Kc1  Bf5  All Black has to do is wait until White runs out of pawn moves; then he will be forced to move either the king or the knight.  80.h4  Bg6  81.h5  Bxh5  82.Nc2  Be8  83.Na1  Ba4  84.Nc2  Kb3  0-1. }
1... a3
{ 74...axb3  75.Kd2 =  fortress   74...Kc4  75.Na5+  Kb5  76.Nb3  repeats the position }
2. bxa3 bxa3 3. Kd2 Kc4 4. Na5+ Kb4 5. Nc6+ Kb3 6. Na5+ Kb2 7. Nc4+ *

