{ White just played 43. Be3, dominating the knight. Can Black be saved ? } 1...
Kd6
{ The answer is yes, as long as Black can force the white pawn to move to b6, building Fortress I.5. Black starts with the right move, moving the king with a double intention. }
2. Kf5
{ 44.b6 ?!  makes things easier, because it is enough for Black to hide his king in the Fortress. In this case it is not necessary to reach a8 - it is enough to get to c8:  Kd7 = }
2... Kd5 3. b6
{ Giri made this move with little hope, after checking that the alternatives did not win either. However there are some funny variations:   45.Kg4  Kc4  46.Bc5 !?  Nf2+ !  47.Bxf2  Kxb4  48.b6  . Now we have a fun position. Black can reach Fortress I.5, but he needs to play correctly with his king:  Kc4 !   48...Kb5 ?  49.Kf5  Kc6  50.Ke6  loses  49.Kf5  Kd5  with a draw as the black king reaches c8. We return to the game. To the surprise and disbelief of all the fans around the world, Shankland resigned here. He thought he had to get his king on a8 to draw, but in this position Black just needs to take his king to c8. }
3... Kd6 4. Kg4 Kd7 5. Kxh3 Kc8
{ There is no way to separate the black king from the b7-pawn without forcing stalemate. }
